UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Coenzymes fall into two groups: loosely bound coenzymes, which dissociate and act as substrates, and tightly or covalently bound coenzymes (prosthetic groups), which stay attached to the enzyme. Because a covalently bound coenzyme cannot leave, once it has reacted the enzyme cannot use it in another round of catalysis until the coenzyme's initial state is reformed, which requires an additional chemical reaction. FAD/FADH₂ is a case in point: because the flavin is tightly or covalently attached to the enzyme, FAD must be regenerated in each catalytic cycle. You must reason from the covalent attachment to what the enzyme needs before it can turn over again. (Choice A) is correct: because the flavin is covalently tethered it cannot be swapped out, so the enzyme's spent FADH₂ must be reset in place, re-oxidized back to FAD by a separate electron acceptor, before another identical cycle can occur. That is exactly the in-place regeneration required for a covalently bound FAD. (Choice B) is incorrect: dissociating and replacing the coenzyme from a free pool is how a loosely bound co-substrate behaves; a covalently bound flavin is physically incapable of leaving to be exchanged, so this route is unavailable to the enzyme. (Choice C) is incorrect: the apoenzyme is by definition the protein without its cofactor, so it cannot 'release the flavin,' and enzymes do not unfold and re-fold between turnovers; restoring activity requires chemical regeneration of the coenzyme, not reassembly of the protein. (Choice D) is incorrect: the enzyme is not switched off by a regulatory conformational change but is chemically 'used up' at the flavin (now FADH₂); allosteric activators modulate activity at a separate site and cannot re-oxidize a reduced coenzyme. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the consequence of tight/covalent coenzyme binding (the coenzyme must be regenerated on the enzyme rather than exchanged) to determine what a second catalytic cycle requires.$px$ WHERE id = '4f93bedf-5397-4ba5-8ecc-c8c66d75e95d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because the low-myoglobin, few-mitochondria, quick-to-fatigue profile of fiber Y identifies a fast glycolytic fiber, the type that relies on anaerobic glycolysis to drive rapid, forceful contractions. Fast glycolytic (FG) fibers use anaerobic glycolysis as their primary ATP source and carry high glycogen stores that are broken down in glycolysis to generate ATP quickly and produce high levels of tension, but they contain few mitochondria and little myoglobin. Because they generate ATP quickly but not efficiently, they fatigue rapidly and can be used only for short periods, making them well suited to producing rapid, forceful contractions for quick, powerful movements. That is precisely the demand of an explosive sprint, in contrast to the intermediate role of fast oxidative (FO) fibers. Fiber Y's data signature (low myoglobin, few mitochondria, short time to fatigue) is the unique fingerprint of this fiber. (Choice A) is wrong: fiber W's abundant myoglobin and many mitochondria mark it as a slow oxidative fiber, which does not produce a large amount of tension and is not used for powerful, fast movements that require high amounts of energy and rapid cross-bridge cycling, so aerobic output cannot supply a maximal burst. (Choice B) is wrong: fiber X (low myoglobin, many mitochondria, intermediate fatigue) is a fast oxidative fiber suited to sustained movements like walking, which require less energy than an explosive movement such as sprinting; it is the runner-up but not the most direct power source for an explosive sprint. (Choice C) is wrong: it again selects the slow oxidative fiber W, and high mitochondrial density supports endurance, not peak power. Aerobic metabolism occurs in the mitochondria and yields fatigue resistance, not the rapid high-tension output of glycolytic fibers. (Choice D) is correct: only the fast glycolytic profile couples anaerobic glycolysis to the quick, high-tension, short-duration contraction an explosive sprint requires. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated fiber properties to infer the underlying metabolic mechanism and predict which fiber meets the contractile demand.$px$ WHERE id = '4fb380e5-4aa8-4485-aabb-e6c111a8cf20';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.'

The answer is A because the column separates proteins by their ability to form a specific, non-covalent complex with the ligand attached to the beads. Only protein R binds biotin (and it does so with an extraordinarily high affinity, Kd = \(1\times10^{-15}\) M), so only R is held on the column while the wash buffer carries the non-binding proteins away. Because the interaction is reversible and specific, R can then be displaced by flooding the column with free biotin, which competes for R's binding site and releases it into the buffer.

(Choice A) is correct: Retention on this column depends on recognizing and binding the immobilized ligand. R is the only protein with measurable biotin affinity, so it is the only one that resists the wash and is later eluted by free biotin.

(Choice B) is incorrect: A protein's size does not determine retention here. Separation by the ability of large molecules to pass around beads while small ones enter the pores describes size-based (gel-filtration) behavior, not this ligand-based column. Protein T has no biotin affinity and washes straight through despite being the largest.

(Choice C) is incorrect: Retention on this column is not driven by electrostatic attraction to the resin. Charge-based binding to a charged resin describes a different technique; the agarose here is functionalized with biotin, and S has no biotin affinity, so its negative charge does not keep it on the column. It washes through.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Being trapped inside porous beads by small size again describes a size-based mechanism, not specific ligand binding. Q neither binds biotin nor is retained by size, so it elutes in the wash regardless of its low molecular weight.

To answer this question, you needed to apply your understanding of how an immobilized ligand selectively captures its binding partner to a novel data set, identifying which protein is retained based on its affinity rather than its size or charge.$px$ WHERE id = '4fc155ca-7509-48e1-b16c-9109ecb6d85a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Biosignaling.'

The answer is A because the mutation removes Gsα's intrinsic GTPase step, so once Gsα is GTP-bound it cannot switch itself off; it persistently stimulates adenylate cyclase and keeps cAMP high independent of what the receptor is doing. The data already show this: the Mut line sits at ~470 nM cAMP with no hormone (versus 15 nM for WT), meaning the pathway is locked "on" upstream of the effector enzyme and downstream of the receptor. Because the antagonist acts only at the receptor's hormone-binding pocket, and the constitutively active Gsα no longer depends on receptor input, blocking the receptor cannot lower the signal. cAMP stays near its already-elevated ~470 nM.

(Choice A) is correct: The GTP-bound Gsα is the active state, and losing the hydrolysis step leaves it "continually activated." Its stimulation of cyclase is uncoupled from receptor occupancy, so a receptor-level antagonist leaves cAMP essentially unchanged and high.

(Choice B) is incorrect: This treats receptor occupancy as the required, rate-limiting input to Gsα. That is true in WT, but the mutation places the lesion downstream of the receptor; a Gsα stuck in its GTP-bound form does not need the receptor to load nucleotide, so blocking the receptor does not return cAMP to the 15 nM baseline.

(Choice C) is incorrect: It reverses the activity rule for G-proteins. The GTP-bound form is the active form and the GDP-bound form is inactive; a mutant that cannot hydrolyze GTP is trapped in the active GTP-bound state, not an inactive one. The measured 470 nM cAMP in the unstimulated Mut line directly contradicts the claim that no signal was ever generated.

(Choice D) is incorrect: No hormone-dependent negative feedback on adenylate cyclase is described in this pathway, and the "+ hormone" Mut value (495 nM) is barely above its no-hormone value (470 nM), showing the system is already near-maximally driven. Removing the antagonist's target (the receptor) provides no mechanism to push output above the hormone-stimulated ceiling.

As a reasoning (Skill 2) task, success requires locating the perturbation within the cascade, recognizing that a GTPase-dead Gsα is constitutively active and lies downstream of the receptor, and then predicting that a receptor-level intervention cannot control a signal that has been uncoupled from the receptor, rather than restating any single definition.$px$ WHERE id = '509fc468-0330-4c93-b07e-f41b887a286e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' A natural catastrophe that kills a large fraction of a population at random magnifies genetic drift through what is known as the bottleneck effect; the survivors' genetic structure becomes the whole population's structure and may differ substantially from the pre-disaster population. Because the flood swept beetles away indiscriminately, the surviving allele frequencies are simply a chance sample, not the result of any color-based advantage. In genetic drift, some individuals leave more offspring not because of an advantage conferred by a genetically-encoded trait but purely because of luck. (Choice A) is correct: with survival determined by where a beetle happened to be rather than by its color, the jump in the green allele from 0.50 to 0.80 is chance sampling (a bottleneck-driven episode of genetic drift), not evidence that green conferred a fitness advantage. (Choice B) is incorrect: natural selection requires differential reproduction tied to a heritable trait, but the stem states the flood killed beetles indiscriminately, so a rise in green allele frequency does not indicate that green beetles survived better. Assuming any frequency change equals selection is a common error. (Choice C) is incorrect: this describes the founder effect, which occurs when a small group leaves to start a new population in a new location; here the survivors remain in place after a catastrophe reduces the existing population, which is a bottleneck, not a founder event. (Choice D) is incorrect: a bottleneck suddenly wipes out much of the gene pool and therefore reduces genetic variation, and small surviving populations are more susceptible to further drift, so variation decreases rather than increases. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of genetic drift and the bottleneck effect to allele-frequency data and distinguish chance-driven change from fitness-driven selection.$px$ WHERE id = '509fc911-18a8-47b0-b4b2-33a75fb04592';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' Microtubules are hollow tubes built from α- and β-tubulin dimers that "can disassemble and reform quickly," and this dynamic behavior lets them "pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell." A drug that blocks depolymerization freezes the existing microtubules but does not destroy them, so processes that need microtubules to shorten fail, while processes that only need an intact track (or that do not use microtubules at all) continue. (Choice A) is correct: pulling separated chromosomes to the poles depends on the attached spindle microtubules shortening; because the drug prevents that disassembly, the microtubules cannot reel the chromosomes inward, so this movement is the event that fails. (Choice B) is incorrect: motor-driven vesicle transport rides along the surface of an intact microtubule, and the drug leaves that track in place (it may even stabilize it), so the motor protein can still walk its cargo along the tubule. (Choice C) is incorrect: the 9 + 2 microtubule core of a cilium is already a stable, assembled structure, so preventing depolymerization does not dismantle it, and ciliary beating is driven by motor proteins that remain intact. (Choice D) is incorrect: anchoring the nucleus is the job of intermediate filaments, a separate cytoskeletal element whose structural role is independent of tubulin, so a microtubule-specific drug does not disrupt it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of microtubule dynamic instability to predict which process breaks when depolymerization, but not the track itself, is blocked.$px$ WHERE id = '50ba0a8e-a7bc-4188-88fd-5b20bc648fe9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because, with oxygen gone and no alternative inorganic electron acceptor supplied, E. coli cannot run an electron transport chain and must turn to fermentation, which produces ATP only by substrate-level phosphorylation during glycolysis. E. coli is a facultative anaerobe: when an appropriate final electron acceptor is available it respires, because respiration allows much greater ATP production per glucose, but when none is present NADH must be reoxidized to NAD+ through fermentation. Fermentation involves no electron transport system and does not directly produce any ATP beyond that made during glycolysis by substrate-level phosphorylation, giving a maximum of two ATP molecules per glucose during glycolysis. The aerobic phase, by contrast, reaches a theoretical maximum near 38 ATP per glucose, so removing oxygen without an alternative acceptor collapses the yield from ~38 to ~2. (Choice A) Glycolysis by itself nets only two ATP per glucose, not 38; the figure of ~38 comes from the full respiratory pathway including oxidative phosphorylation, so this choice misattributes the whole-pathway total to glycolysis. (Choice B) Anaerobic respiration does use an electron transport chain, but it requires a non-oxygen inorganic final electron acceptor (such as nitrate), and none is supplied here; moreover even when it occurs it generates smaller electrochemical gradients, so less ATP is formed through anaerobic respiration, and it neither happens in this setup nor matches the aerobic yield. (Choice C) Fermentation regenerates NAD+ to keep glycolysis running, but it adds no ATP beyond glycolysis itself and cannot exceed the aerobic yield; it produces very little ATP, only two ATP molecules per glucose, so the yield falls rather than rises. (Choice D) Correct: lacking any electron acceptor, the cells ferment and net only the ~2 ATP per glucose made by substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the facultative-anaerobe metabolic rules to a specific change in oxygen availability and predict the quantitative energy-yield consequence rather than recall a single fact.$px$ WHERE id = '50cd91cb-ed01-4fc8-89a3-0f70915df935';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Ions in Solutions (GC).' A solution conducts electricity only because it contains freely mobile charged particles, so brightness tracks the concentration of ions actually present. At equal molarity, the deciding factor is how many moles of mobile ions each dissolved formula unit releases: a strong electrolyte that dissociates completely into more ions per formula unit produces the greatest ion concentration and therefore the strongest conduction. (Choice D) is correct: \(\text{CaCl}_2\) is a soluble ionic compound that dissociates essentially completely into three ions per formula unit (\(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) plus two \(\text{Cl}^-\)), so a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution yields roughly \(0.30\ \text{M}\) total ions, more mobile charge carriers than any of the others, so the bulb glows most brightly. (Choice A) is incorrect: glucose is a molecular nonelectrolyte that dissolves as intact neutral molecules and produces no ions, so its solution cannot conduct and the bulb stays dark; assuming any dissolved solute conducts overlooks that conduction requires charged species. (Choice B) is incorrect: acetic acid is a weak electrolyte that only partially ionizes, so a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution contains only a small fraction of ions and conducts weakly; treating every acid as a strong conductor ignores partial ionization. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{NaCl}\) is a strong electrolyte, but it dissociates into only two ions per formula unit (\(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\)), giving about \(0.20\ \text{M}\) total ions, fewer mobile charges than \(\text{CaCl}_2\) at the same molarity, so it conducts well but not as strongly. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between electrolyte strength, the number of ions released per formula unit, and solution conductivity to rank four solutions rather than recall a single fact.$px$ WHERE id = '50d17ea8-41bc-405b-9174-5ebb76aa92ef';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior'. The answer to this question is B because the comparison must be split into two regimes by first counting electrons. \(\text{O}^{2-}\) has \(8 + 2 = 10\) electrons, \(\text{Na}^+\) has \(11 - 1 = 10\), and \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) has \(12 - 2 = 10\), so these three are isoelectronic with neon and their outermost electrons all reside in the \(n = 2\) shell. \(\text{Cl}^-\), however, has \(17 + 1 = 18\) electrons and an occupied \(n = 3\) shell. The number of occupied principal shells is the dominant size factor when it differs: \(\text{Cl}^-\) carries an entire additional shell, so it is the largest ion in the set regardless of nuclear charge. Within the isoelectronic \(n = 2\) subset, shell count and electron-electron repulsion are held constant, so the only variable is nuclear charge; a larger \(Z\) raises \(Z_\text{eff}\) and contracts the cloud, giving \(\text{O}^{2-}\) (\(Z = 8\)) > \(\text{Na}^+\) (\(Z = 11\)) > \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) (\(Z = 12\)). Chaining the two regimes yields \(\text{Cl}^- > \text{O}^{2-} > \text{Na}^+ > \text{Mg}^{2+}\), making B correct. (Choice A) This treats all four ions as a single isoelectronic series and ranks by nuclear charge alone, placing \(\text{Cl}^-\) (\(Z = 17\)) last as the smallest. It drops the fact that \(\text{Cl}^-\) is not isoelectronic with the other three; its added \(n = 3\) shell makes it the largest, so the within-shell \(Z\) rule cannot be applied across the differing electron counts. (Choice C) This correctly places \(\text{Cl}^-\) largest on the strength of its extra shell but then reverses the isoelectronic trend within the \(n = 2\) subset, treating higher nuclear charge as producing a larger ion. At fixed electron count a greater proton number raises \(Z_\text{eff}\) and shrinks the radius, so \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) is the smallest of that subset, not the largest. (Choice D) This ranks by ionic charge, pairing the \(2-\) and \(1-\) anions ahead of the \(1+\) and \(2+\) cations and ordering by charge magnitude, which puts \(\text{O}^{2-}\) first and \(\text{Cl}^-\) second. Charge sign and magnitude do not set size across species with different shell counts; the occupied-shell difference makes \(\text{Cl}^-\) larger than the \(n = 2\) anion \(\text{O}^{2-}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify the controlling constraint (which ions share an electron count) and then chain the principal-shell rule with the isoelectronic nuclear-charge trend to produce a single ordering.$px$ WHERE id = '50d636ea-09f3-4be3-b70e-749074d61d84';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is D because parathyroid hormone raises blood calcium in part by promoting osteoclast activity, and osteoclasts break down (resorb) the mineralized bone matrix, liberating the calcium stored there into the circulation. (Choice D) is correct: PTH increases the number and activity of osteoclasts, the bone-resorbing cells; as they dissolve the mineralized matrix, calcium and phosphate are released from bone into the blood, which raises serum calcium toward normal. (Choice A) is incorrect: this describes the opposite (reversed) action. Osteoblasts deposit calcium into new matrix, which would lower blood calcium, whereas PTH's net skeletal effect is to mobilize calcium out of bone. (Choice B) is incorrect: PTH does not act by directly mineralizing osteoid; mineralization deposits calcium into bone and would lower, not raise, serum calcium, and PTH's calcium-raising bone effect is resorptive rather than mineralizing. (Choice C) is incorrect: PTH increases bone remodeling toward net resorption rather than suppressing turnover; suppression of resorption to conserve matrix calcium describes calcitonin's action, which lowers blood calcium and is functionally opposite to PTH. This question exercises mechanistic reasoning about how a hormone's effect on a specific cell type (osteoclasts) produces a whole-body change in calcium homeostasis.$px$ WHERE id = '512684d6-245e-4da3-826c-02edbe9d82ed';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.'

The answer is Z because the two axes of a 2D gel encode two independent properties, and the upper-right corner requires a specific pairing of both. In the first dimension (isoelectric focusing), each protein migrates until it reaches the position where the gel pH equals its \(pI\), where its net charge is zero and migration halts. With pH 3 at the left and pH 10 at the right, a protein with a high (basic) \(pI\) focuses toward the right edge, while a low (acidic) \(pI\) focuses toward the left. In the second dimension (SDS-PAGE), SDS coats every protein with uniform negative charge density so migration depends only on mass: large proteins are retarded by the gel matrix and stay near the top, while small proteins run far toward the bottom. "Upper-right" therefore demands high mass (top) AND high \(pI\) (right). Only Z satisfies both, with a \(pI\) of 8.6 (right) and a mass of 71 kDa (top).

(Choice A) is correct: Z has \(pI = 8.6\) (basic → focuses right) and mass 71 kDa (large → migrates little, stays near the top), placing it in the upper-right corner.

(Choice B) is incorrect: W is indeed large (68 kDa, so near the top), but its \(pI\) of 4.2 is acidic, so it focuses toward the left (pH 3) edge, not the right. It would appear in the upper-LEFT corner. This choice correctly reads the mass axis but inverts the direction of the pH-gradient axis.

(Choice C) is incorrect: X has a basic \(pI\) (8.9 → focuses right), but at only 15 kDa it is small and would migrate far down the SDS gel to sit near the BOTTOM, not the top. This choice gets the right side correct but reverses the mass-position relationship (small proteins run farther, not less).

(Choice D) is incorrect: Y is both small (14 kDa → near the bottom) and acidic (\(pI = 4.5\) → focuses left), so it would sit in the lower-LEFT corner, the opposite corner from the observed spot on both axes.

At the Skill 2 level, you cannot recall a fact to answer this; you must map each protein's two measured values onto the two orthogonal separation axes and recognize that the upper-right position is defined by the conjunction of high mass and high \(pI\), then test each candidate against both criteria simultaneously.$px$ WHERE id = '5210c10f-2694-4da7-8d70-ad9d61f90e06';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is D because target-tissue resistance means peripheral cells respond poorly to insulin even though the beta cells still produce it; the pancreas actually increases insulin secretion in compensation, so the hallmark of a resistance-driven mechanism is elevated (not deficient) fasting insulin persisting alongside high blood glucose. This directly contrasts with the child's autoimmune destruction of the insulin-producing beta cells, which causes an absolute lack of the hormone. (Choice D) is correct: in insulin resistance the beta cells remain intact and hypersecrete insulin to overcome poor target-cell responsiveness, so measurable, even elevated, insulin coexists with hyperglycemia, evidence that the defect lies downstream at the target tissue rather than in hormone supply. (Choice B) is incorrect: undetectable fasting insulin indicates an absolute deficiency of the hormone, which is exactly the child's autoimmune beta-cell loss, not a target-tissue responsiveness problem. (Choice C) is incorrect: glucagon, secreted by the alpha cells, raises blood glucose by promoting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, so elevated glucagon would implicate a counter-regulatory hormone excess rather than impaired insulin action at target tissues. (Choice A) is incorrect: somatostatin from the delta cells inhibits both insulin and glucagon release; reduced somatostatin does not describe reduced target-tissue responsiveness to insulin and is not a recognized cause of resistance-driven hyperglycemia. This item requires Skill 2 reasoning because the student must interpret laboratory data to infer an underlying mechanism and distinguish an absolute hormone-supply deficit from a downstream target-tissue defect.$px$ WHERE id = '52471af3-9be7-42b8-8130-a84a28ac0eb5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' Evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness is not a measure of how long, large, or vigorous an individual is; it is an organism's relative contribution to the gene pool of the next generation. Natural selection favors individuals with greater contributions to that gene pool, and fitness reflects the capacity to produce more reproductively viable offspring relative to others. The decisive quantity is therefore the number of offspring that themselves survive to reproduce, because only descendants who reach reproductive age actually propagate an individual's alleles forward. (Choice B) is correct: Male Q leaves 7 offspring that survive to breed, the most of any male, so it contributes the largest share of alleles to the next generation's gene pool and has the highest fitness, even though it lived the shortest time and was the smallest. (Choice A) is incorrect: Male P has the longest lifespan, but longevity is not fitness; despite fathering many eggs, only 3 of its offspring survive to reproduce, a smaller genetic contribution than Q. (Choice C) is incorrect: Male R fathered the most eggs (25), but raw fecundity is not fitness. The 25 eggs yield only 4 breeding offspring, so R passes fewer alleles forward than Q. (Choice D) is incorrect: Male S is the heaviest, but a robust individual trait does not equal fitness; S leaves only 2 offspring that survive to breed, the smallest contribution to the next generation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of Darwinian fitness (relative genetic contribution to the next generation) to a data table and distinguish it from survival, body size, and raw fecundity.$px$ WHERE id = '52837759-a4fc-4d66-a6c7-dfaf4121025e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Elements in the same group share the same number and type of valence electrons, namely those in the highest principal quantum number (\(n\)) shell. They differ only in which shell \(n\) that valence pattern occupies. For a main-group element, a completely filled inner \(d^{10}\) subshell counts as core, not valence, so the valence count is read only from the outermost \(ns\) and \(np\) electrons. Phosphorus sits in group 15 with valence \(3s^2 3p^3\): five valence electrons. A group-mate must therefore be a *different* element that also has five valence electrons of the \(ns^2 np^3\) type.\n\n(Choice B) is correct: \([\text{Kr}]\,5s^2\,4d^{10}\,5p^3\) is antimony (Sb). The filled \(4d^{10}\) is core, so its valence shell is \(5s^2 5p^3\), which is five valence electrons in the \(ns^2 np^3\) pattern. That reproduces phosphorus's valence configuration at \(n=5\), placing antimony in group 15, so it is a genuine group-mate.\n\n(Choice A) is incorrect: \(1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^3\) is simply the full configuration of phosphorus itself (\(Z=15\)). Although its \(3p^3\) ending superficially matches the group, it is the *same* element, not a different one in the same group, so the question's requirement of a different element excludes it.\n\n(Choice C) is incorrect: \([\text{Ne}]\,3s^2 3p^5\) is chlorine, whose valence \(ns^2 np^5\) gives seven valence electrons. Two extra \(p\) electrons place it in group 17 (the halogens), not group 15.\n\n(Choice D) is incorrect: \([\text{Ar}]\,4s^2\,3d^{10}\,4p^1\) is gallium; with the \(3d^{10}\) counted as core, its valence is \(4s^2 4p^1\), giving only three valence electrons and placing it in group 13, not group 15.\n\nThis is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that group membership is fixed by the number and type of valence (highest-\(n\)) electrons, that a filled \(d^{10}\) subshell is core rather than valence, and that a group-mate must be a distinct element sharing phosphorus's \(ns^2 np^3\) pattern.$px$ WHERE id = '52942f5c-e1cf-4a2e-9874-038e68b26109';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development.' The fast block to polyspermy is the "wave of negativity" first described by Ernest Everett Just: within about a second of the first sperm binding, the oocyte's membrane potential changes and additional sperm can no longer fuse, well before the slower structural changes to the egg coat set in. Mechanistically this fast block is a depolarization, an influx of positive ions (sodium) that shifts the membrane potential upward from its resting negative value. The depolarized membrane is unreceptive to further sperm fusion. (Choice A) is correct: an inward sodium current depolarizes the oocyte membrane almost instantly, and this depolarization is the fast block itself, blocking further fusion within the first second. (Choice B) is incorrect: it is also a fast, membrane-potential change, but it moves in the wrong direction. The fast block is a depolarization (membrane potential rising toward zero), not a hyperpolarization; potassium efflux driving the potential more negative is not the mechanism that prevents polyspermy. (Choice C) is incorrect: cortical granule fusion and release of zonal inhibiting proteins is the slow block (the cortical reaction), which alters the egg coat over the minute that follows and is not the near-instantaneous first response. (Choice D) is incorrect: acrosomal enzyme release degrades the glycoprotein coat to let a sperm INTO the oocyte, so it promotes fusion rather than blocking additional sperm. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the fast block to polyspermy is a rapid membrane depolarization driven by cation influx, and distinguish that specific ionic event from a hyperpolarization, the cortical reaction, and the acrosomal reaction.$px$ WHERE id = '52a652cb-8719-4cc5-b0a5-7f28eb642f48';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because compliance is defined as the change in volume divided by the change in transpulmonary pressure (C = ΔV/ΔP); a stiff, fibrotic lung that demands a larger pressure change (5 cm H2O) to achieve the identical 0.5 L volume change has a smaller ΔV/ΔP ratio and therefore lower compliance than the healthy lung, which needs only 2 cm H2O for the same volume. (Choice D) is correct: it pairs the correct direction (lower compliance) with the correct reasoning. More pressure is required per unit of volume added, which is exactly a reduced ΔV/ΔP. (Choice A) is incorrect: although its reasoning (larger pressure change needed for the same volume) is true, it draws the wrong conclusion; requiring more pressure per unit volume is the hallmark of LOW compliance (high elastance), not high compliance. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reaches the correct conclusion (lower compliance) but with a false premise. The fibrotic lung does not produce a larger volume change for the same pressure; it produces a smaller volume change per unit pressure. (Choice C) is incorrect: both parts are wrong. The fibrotic lung is less compliant, not more, and it yields a smaller (not larger) volume change for a given pressure change. This question tests the skill of applying the quantitative definition of compliance (ΔV/ΔP) to distinguish a stiff, low-compliance lung from a normal one.$px$ WHERE id = '52bcfac9-0c2d-45df-ab4f-8a1517e74acf';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development.' Cleavage is a series of mitotic divisions that subdivides the zygote into smaller cells called blastomeres without any cell growth. In humans and other deuterostomes, cleavage is indeterminate: early blastomeres are not yet committed to a particular fate, so removing or separating individual blastomeres does not cause the loss of structures, and each separated cluster can develop into a complete embryo. This uncommitted, still-potent state of the early cells is exactly what allows a single embryo to split and yield two whole, genetically identical individuals. (Choice A) is correct: because determination has not yet occurred at the cleavage stage, each blastomere still retains the developmental potential to build an entire embryo, so a separated cluster can regenerate all parts and form a complete twin. (Choice B) is incorrect: at the cleavage stage the blastomeres have not yet differentiated into tissue precursors; differentiation into specialized tissues comes later, and it is precisely the absence of early commitment, not reorganization of already-specialized cells, that permits each cluster to form a whole individual. (Choice C) is incorrect: the primary germ layers (endoderm, mesoderm, ectoderm) are not established until gastrulation, which occurs well after cleavage; a split relying on pre-formed germ layers describes a later, more committed embryo that could not readily give rise to two complete individuals. (Choice D) is incorrect: fertilization produces a single diploid zygote with one nucleus, and twinning results from separation of blastomeres during cleavage, not from partitioning of two nuclei that were somehow present from the start. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of cell determination to infer that the early embryo's cells must be developmentally uncommitted for a split to yield two complete individuals.$px$ WHERE id = '52ee2eb0-3eee-499f-abb0-402b7c2d888e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is B because an acute, short-term stressor triggers the sympathomedullary (SAM) pathway, prompting the adrenal medulla's chromaffin cells to release the catecholamines epinephrine and norepinephrine into circulation; these hormones increase heart rate, dilate the airways to raise blood oxygen, and signal the liver and skeletal muscle to convert glycogen into glucose, raising blood glucose to fuel immediate exertion. (Choice B) is correct: rising heart rate, bronchodilation, and elevated blood glucose are precisely the systemic effects of medullary catecholamines during the fight-or-flight (alarm) reaction. (Choice A) is incorrect: a slowed heart rate, constricted airways, and falling blood glucose describe the opposite, rest-and-digest direction (parasympathetic tone plus insulin action), which is suppressed, not activated, during acute stress. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly pairs elevated glucose with stress but reverses the cardiac and respiratory effects; catecholamines speed the heart and open the airways rather than slowing the heart and narrowing bronchioles. (Choice D) is incorrect: sodium retention, expanded blood volume, and raised pressure are effects of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex (a mineralocorticoid governing fluid balance) acting over minutes to hours, not the seconds-scale medullary catecholamines that drive the rapid exertion response. Reasoning from the described acute perturbation to the coordinated set of downstream target-organ effects, rather than recalling a single fact, is the Skill 2 demand here.$px$ WHERE id = '53019740-1129-4f29-a478-4d6268ff630d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is B because the embryonic trophoblast secretes human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which "directs the corpus luteum to survive, enlarge, and continue producing progesterone," so the corpus luteum does not regress on its usual schedule. In a non-fertilized cycle, "if pregnancy does not occur within 10 to 12 days, the corpus luteum will stop secreting progesterone and degrade into the corpus albicans," and progesterone falls; the only thing that changes that default in early pregnancy is the rescue signal from the conceptus. The text states explicitly that "if an embryo implants, signals are sent to the corpus luteum to continue secreting progesterone... If an embryo does not implant, no signal is sent to the corpus luteum and it degrades." Thus the single conceptus-derived variable that sustains progesterone is hCG rescuing the corpus luteum. (Choice A) is wrong because the LH surge is the transient trigger of luteinization at ovulation, not a sustained pregnancy signal; progesterone-driven negative feedback actually keeps LH low during the luteal phase. (Choice B) is correct because hCG is the embryonic signal that maintains the corpus luteum and its progesterone output past the normal 10-to-12-day window. (Choice C) is wrong because luteal-phase progesterone suppresses FSH and LH so no new dominant follicle develops; FSH only rises again once the corpus luteum has already degraded. (Choice D) is wrong because without progesterone the spiral arteries constrict and rupture during menses. They are targets of progesterone withdrawal, not a progesterone source. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires contrasting two physiological scenarios and isolating the single causal variable (the hCG rescue signal) that explains the divergent progesterone trajectories rather than recalling one fact.$px$ WHERE id = '531ba8f1-4a2d-47df-8ce5-2cf75b546efd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because at rest tropomyosin winds around the actin filament and physically covers the myosin-binding sites, blocking cross-bridge formation. Tropomyosin winds around the chains of the actin filament and covers the myosin-binding sites so that actin cannot bind myosin, and the troponin-tropomyosin complex keeps the myosin heads from binding to the active sites on the actin microfilaments. Only when \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) rises and binds troponin does tropomyosin slide away from the binding sites on the actin strands, exposing the sites; at rest, with \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) low, tropomyosin stays in place and the heads cannot attach. (Choice A) Troponin bound to \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) describes the activating, not the resting, state. \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding to troponin is the first step of contraction that moves tropomyosin aside, so a \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-occupied troponin would expose the sites rather than block them. (Choice B) The myosin heads project from the thick filament and are the structures held off; they are not part of the thin filament and are not sequestered along actin, so this reverses which filament does the blocking. (Choice C) This reverses the actual relationship: tropomyosin (with troponin) covers sites on actin, not the other way around. Actin does not shield troponin's regulatory site, which is instead where \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds. (Choice D) Tropomyosin covering the myosin-binding sites on actin is the precise resting-state block, the structural reason a low-\(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) fiber stays relaxed. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the resting-state role of tropomyosin in covering actin's myosin-binding sites.$px$ WHERE id = '536ed0b4-c6f4-46e0-8471-dbcbf8dbcd1c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) respond to arterial PO2, but their firing rate barely changes over the normal-to-mild range (roughly 100 down to ~60 mmHg) where hemoglobin stays near-fully saturated; only once PO2 falls onto the steep portion of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve does firing, and therefore ventilation, climb sharply. In contrast, central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond continuously and sensitively to small changes in CO2 (via CSF H+), which is why a 3 mmHg PCO2 rise immediately drives ventilation. This is precisely why CO2, not O2, is the primary breathing stimulus under normal conditions, with O2 becoming a significant drive only during severe hypoxemia. (Choice B) is correct: it correctly links the flat ventilatory response to the near-saturation plateau of the dissociation curve and identifies continuous central CO2 sensitivity as the normal drive. (Choice A) is incorrect: peripheral chemoreceptors respond to PO2, and the response is decidedly non-linear (flat until PO2 ~60 mmHg, then steep), not a small-but-linear signal. Henry's Law describes dissolved gas but does not make ventilation track PO2 linearly here. (Choice C) is incorrect: central chemoreceptors sense CO2/H+, not O2; the O2 (hypoxic) drive is mediated by peripheral, not central, chemoreceptors. (Choice D) is incorrect: scrubbing CO2 can lower H+ (mild alkalosis) which does modestly blunt peripheral output, but the dominant reason ventilation stays flat is the near-flat PO2-firing relationship over the high-saturation range, not a pH gate that is 'overridden'; this option elevates a secondary effect to the primary cause. Skill: This item requires integrating physiological data (a threshold-like ventilatory response) with the underlying chemoreceptor mechanism rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '53b4ff08-0f0a-4ff1-bf88-9e8688ecff5f';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher measures the collapsing (deflating) pressure of an excised lung under two conditions. When the lung is inflated with air, the total inward pressure driving deflation is 8.0 cm H2O. When the same lung is inflated to the identical volume with saline, which abolishes any air-liquid interface, the inward pressure is 3.0 cm H2O. Which condition would MOST closely reproduce, or exceed, the air-filled measurement if surfactant were washed out of the air-filled lung?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the total inward (deflating) pressure of the air-filled lung is the sum of two components: the tissue's intrinsic elastic recoil and the surface tension at the air-liquid interface. The saline-filled measurement (3.0 cm H2O) abolishes the air-liquid interface, so it isolates elastic recoil alone; the difference (8.0 - 3.0 = 5.0 cm H2O) is the surface-tension contribution present in the air-filled lung. Surfactant normally LOWERS that surface tension. Washing surfactant out therefore raises surface tension, increasing the surface-tension component above its normal 5.0 cm H2O value while elastic recoil (3.0 cm H2O) is unchanged. The total deflating pressure thus rises above the original 8.0 cm H2O. (Choice A) is correct: elastic recoil (3.0) is unchanged and the surface-tension term grows beyond 5.0, pushing the total past 8.0. (Choice B) is incorrect: 3.0 cm H2O is the elastic-recoil-only value, but surfactant removal does not eliminate tissue elasticity. It acts on the surface-tension term, and removing surfactant raises tension rather than removing it. (Choice C) is incorrect: 5.0 cm H2O is the normal surface-tension component computed from the difference, but it wrongly assumes elastic recoil vanishes and that tension stays at its surfactant-lowered value; in fact the 3.0 recoil persists and the tension rises. (Choice D) is incorrect: surfactant does not create surface tension. The air-liquid interface does; surfactant reduces it, so removing surfactant increases, not decreases, the deflating pressure. Skill: this item tests the ability to partition a measured physiological quantity into its mechanistic components and predict how perturbing one component changes the whole.$px$ WHERE id = '53b7f902-160c-41bf-b176-124354bdc673';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' For a protein with multiple binding sites, the Hill coefficient measures the degree of cooperativity, and it can range from 1 (no cooperativity, hyperbolic kinetics) up to the number of binding sites only in the limiting, idealized case of infinite cooperativity in which all sites fill in an all-or-none fashion. Hemoglobin illustrates this: setting the exponent to 2.8 gives the best fit to its four-site oxygen data, and reaching n = 4 would require all four oxygens to bind at once, an infinitely cooperative binding of oxygen, which does not describe real binding. Thus a value between 1 and the site count reflects genuine but sub-maximal positive cooperativity. (Choice A) is correct: with four sites, a Hill coefficient of 2.8 sits between 1 and 4, indicating real positive cooperativity that is less than the all-or-none maximum, exactly as the four-site hemoglobin fit of 2.8 illustrates, and paralleling the real tetrameric Pfk A whose Hill coefficient is 3.3 rather than 4. (Choice B) is incorrect: this misreads the Hill coefficient as a literal count of active sites, but it is an empirical measure of cooperativity determined to obtain the best fit to the binding data, not the number of functional subunits; all four sites are functional. (Choice C) is incorrect: a coefficient of 2.8 is well above 1, so the sites are clearly interacting, not independent. Non-cooperative binding gives a Hill coefficient of 1 and a hyperbolic curve, as the equation reduces to Y = L/[KD + L] when n = 1, which contradicts the observed sigmoidal curve. (Choice D) is incorrect: negative cooperativity corresponds to a Hill coefficient below 1 (each binding event lowers remaining affinity, contrasted with positive cooperativity in the KNF model); a value of 2.8 is greater than 1 and therefore signals positive, not negative, cooperativity, and the maximum of 4 represents infinite cooperativity rather than the threshold between positive and negative. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between the Hill coefficient, the number of binding sites, and the degree of cooperativity, recognizing that 2.8 lies between 1 and 4 and therefore denotes sub-maximal positive cooperativity rather than a site count, independent binding, or negative cooperativity.$px$ WHERE id = '543c5343-4baf-4c36-844d-ed3f68a66bd9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

(Choice D) is correct: a positive Benedict's result requires a free anomeric carbon whose cyclic hemiacetal (or, for a ketose, an isomerizable \(\alpha\)-hydroxymethyl ketone) can reversibly open to expose a carbonyl that reduces \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) to brick-red \(\text{Cu}_2\text{O}\). In the third disaccharide the linkage joins the anomeric carbon of glucose (C1) to the anomeric carbon of fructose (C2). Because both anomeric carbons are committed to the glycosidic bond, each ring is locked as a full acetal and neither can open to a free carbonyl, so the sugar is non-reducing and the tube stays negative.

(Choice A) is incorrect: the first disaccharide links glucose C1 to glucose C4, so only one anomeric carbon (the first glucose's C1) is tied up. The second glucose retains a free anomeric carbon at C1 that can open to an aldehyde, making this sugar reducing and the tube positive.

(Choice B) is incorrect: a galactose ring, like glucose, retains a free hemiacetal that opens to an aldehyde whenever its anomeric carbon is not in the glycosidic bond. Here the linkage uses galactose C1 but leaves the glucose anomeric carbon free, so this disaccharide is reducing; the claim that galactose cannot form an aldehyde is false.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it identifies the right tube but for the wrong reason. Benedict's reagent does react with ketoses such as free fructose, which isomerizes to an aldose under basic conditions; the negative result is not because fructose is a ketose but because the C1-C2 linkage occupies both anomeric carbons.

At the Skill 2 level, you must map each described linkage onto whether an anomeric carbon remains free, then reason that only the disaccharide committing both anomeric carbons to the bond can neither open a ring nor reduce \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\).$px$ WHERE id = '5483a0f3-90ab-4755-9e00-7ffb05801261';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Biosignaling.' The answer is A because a fall in cyclic AMP that requires an intact seven-transmembrane receptor (the enzyme is unaffected when assayed alone) indicates the receptor couples to an inhibitory G protein whose active α subunit lowers adenylate cyclase output, so co-application must partially oppose the stimulatory β-adrenergic (Gs) response and blunt the cyclic AMP rise. (Choice A) is correct: the drop in cyclic AMP and PKA-substrate phosphorylation, occurring only through the receptor, marks coupling to the inhibitory (Gi) class, whose active \(\alpha\) subunit suppresses adenylate cyclase and therefore counteracts the stimulatory receptor when both are engaged. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the sign of the observed effect. An agonist that lowers cyclic AMP cannot be coupled to a stimulatory (Gs) α subunit, so it would oppose, not add to, the β-adrenergic rise (reversed-relationship misconception). (Choice C) is incorrect: it invokes βγ-only signaling to claim no effect on adenylate cyclase, but the measured decrease in cyclic AMP shows the pathway does modulate the enzyme, so predicting "unchanged" ignores the data (partial-truth misconception that βγ signaling excludes cyclase regulation). (Choice D) is incorrect: it attributes an intrinsic phosphodiesterase activity to the receptor itself; GPCRs act through G proteins on effector enzymes and do not themselves hydrolyze cyclic AMP, and this would predict abolition rather than blunting (process-step confusion). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the opposite effects of stimulatory and inhibitory G proteins on adenylate cyclase to infer receptor coupling from a drug's effect and predict the outcome of combined treatment.$px$ WHERE id = '54da1961-5ea5-4a51-9bde-f57b797329f5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is D because the observation to be explained is that the reducing power of cytosolic \(\text{NADH}\) reaches the electron transport chain while the labeled molecule itself never enters the matrix. That pattern is only consistent with a barrier that stops the whole molecule but not its electrons: the inner mitochondrial membrane is impermeable to \(\text{NADH}\), so a shuttle hands the electrons across and regenerates cytosolic \(\text{NAD}^+\) while the original \(\text{NADH}\) stays in the cytosol.

(Choice D) is correct: it matches both facts in the scenario: the molecule stays out (membrane impermeability) yet its electrons get in (a shuttle carries the reducing equivalents only).

(Choice A) is incorrect: if \(\text{NADH}\) freely diffused in and were re-oxidized inside, the labeled molecule would still be detected transiently in the matrix, contradicting the observation that it never appears there; the inner membrane is in fact impermeable to \(\text{NADH}\).

(Choice B) is incorrect: cytosolic \(\text{NADH}\) is not converted into a free \(\text{FADH}_2\) molecule that then diffuses across; \(\text{FADH}_2\) is enzyme-bound and also does not cross the membrane, so this invents a nonexistent mobile carrier.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the outer membrane is freely permeable to small molecules, and the barrier that matters here is the inner membrane; \(\text{NADH}\) does not pass through the inner membrane into the matrix and then dock onto Complex I from that side.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must infer the need for an electron-only shuttle from the membrane-barrier evidence rather than recall the shuttle's name.$px$ WHERE id = '54e460d3-8f61-441b-bd28-0f85cb71e543';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because the active site is unchanged in the free enzyme and only takes on its complementary shape after the substrate enters, so the conformational change FOLLOWS binding. That is the defining feature of induced fit. In the induced-fit model, contact between the substrate and the protein changes the protein's shape so that it can bind, and this interaction shifts the enzyme's structure into an arrangement that fits the substrate more closely. As the enzyme and substrate come together, their interaction causes a mild shift in the enzyme's structure that produces an ideal binding arrangement. Statements II and III both describe this binding-induced change, so both are correct. (Choice A) I only is wrong because lock-and-key requires the active site to be ALREADY complementary without any further modification to the enzyme's shape; here the shape changes only after binding, so I is false. (Choice B) III only is incomplete: III is true, but it omits II, which states the same induced-fit conclusion. (Choice C) I and III only pairs a true statement (III) with a false one (I); the final complementary fit is real, but it is generated by binding, not preexisting, so lock-and-key (I) is not supported. (Choice D) is correct: II and III only. Both capture that complementarity is induced by binding, the signature of induced fit. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural difference between the two binding models to interpret experimental shape data and assign the correct mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = '54f8a12c-4d35-4435-aee1-93ee155ca2e0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' Prokaryotes reproduce asexually by binary fission, in which one cell replicates its single circular chromosome and pinches into two clones; when nutrients are unlimited the population grows exponentially, doubling once per generation time and tracing a J-shaped curve. The generation (doubling) time here is 20 minutes, so a 2-hour (120-minute) incubation spans \(120 \div 20 = 6\) generations, and each generation multiplies the population by 2, giving a final density of initial \(\times\, 2^{6}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: 1,000 cells/mL treats the population as doubling only once over the entire 2-hour incubation (\(500 \times 2\)), ignoring that each 20-minute generation is a separate doubling. (Choice B) is incorrect: 3,000 cells/mL comes from multiplying the starting count by the number of generations (\(500 \times 6\)), which models linear (additive) growth rather than the accelerating exponential growth that binary fission produces. (Choice C) is correct: over 120 minutes there are 6 generations, so the population is \(500 \times 2^{6} = 500 \times 64 = 32{,}000\) cells/mL. (Choice D) is incorrect: 16,000 cells/mL is \(500 \times 2^{5}\), an off-by-one error that counts only 5 doublings instead of the 6 that fit into 120 minutes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the exponential-growth relationship for binary fission, converting incubation time into a number of generations and raising 2 to that power, rather than recall a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '55fe879e-4c10-4e3d-a607-0f42be615279';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is C because the insulin surge after refeeding is the master anabolic signal: it switches on glycogen and triacylglycerol synthesis and simultaneously switches off the fasting programs of gluconeogenesis and lipolysis, reversing catabolism to anabolism.

(Choice C) is correct: resumed glycogen and triacylglycerol synthesis with gluconeogenesis and lipolysis shut down is the defining catabolic-to-anabolic reversal that insulin produces.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it keeps the fasting (catabolic) program running, which is exactly what the insulin surge terminates.
(Choice B) is incorrect: high insulin suppresses ketogenesis, so ketone production falls, not rises, upon refeeding; ketogenesis also does not dispose of a glucose load.
(Choice D) is incorrect: glucagon falls as insulin rises (the two hormones oppose each other), and glucagon drives glycogenolysis, not storage, so it does not co-drive glycogen storage here.

This item rewards Skill 2 (reasoning) by asking the student to trace how a single hormonal signal flips the direction of multiple pathways at once.$px$ WHERE id = '5815a279-8801-45fe-926d-426be2f47a1f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is C because \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime} = -RT\ln K\) rearranges to \(\ln K = -\Delta G^{\circ\prime}/RT\). In joules, \(\ln K = -(-5700)/[(8.314)(298)] = 5700/2478 \approx 2.30\), so \(K = e^{2.30} \approx 10\). A negative \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) must correspond to K > 1 (products favored).

(Choice C) is correct: \(\ln K \approx 2.3\) gives \(K = e^{2.3} \approx 10\), consistent with a product-favored reaction (\(\Delta G^{\circ\prime} < 0\)).

(Choice A) is incorrect: K ≈ 0.1 comes from a sign error. Dropping the negative in \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime} = -RT\ln K\) gives \(\ln K = -2.3\), but a negative \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) requires K > 1, not < 1.

(Choice B) is incorrect: K ≈ 1 results from leaving \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) in kJ while R is in J (5.7/2478 ≈ 0.0023), collapsing \(\ln K\) to nearly zero; the units must match.

(Choice D) is incorrect: K ≈ 200 uses base-10 (\(10^{2.3}\)) instead of the natural log; because the equation contains \(\ln\), you exponentiate with e, giving ≈ 10.

This item is Skill 4: you must rearrange \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime} = -RT\ln K\), keep units consistent, and exponentiate correctly to extract K.$px$ WHERE id = '58a39476-011f-4ae6-9936-e636a92e2a78';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Ions in Solutions (GC).' The brightness of the bulb reflects electrical conductivity, which depends on the concentration of freely mobile ions in solution; because all four solutes are at the same molarity, the solution that dissociates into the greatest number of ions per formula unit will carry the most current. Strong electrolytes dissociate essentially completely, weak electrolytes ionize only slightly, and nonelectrolytes produce no ions at all. (Choice A) is correct: calcium chloride is a strong electrolyte that dissociates completely into \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and \(2\,\text{Cl}^-\), yielding three moles of ions per mole of solute; at equal molarity this gives the highest concentration of mobile ions and therefore the brightest bulb. (Choice B) is incorrect: sodium chloride is also a strong electrolyte, but it dissociates into only \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\), which is two ions per formula unit, so at the same concentration it produces fewer mobile ions than \(\text{CaCl}_2\) and conducts less well. (Choice C) is incorrect: acetic acid is a weak acid and thus a weak electrolyte, ionizing only about 1% in water, so a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution contains a very low concentration of ions and conducts only weakly despite being an acid. (Choice D) is incorrect: glucose is a molecular nonelectrolyte that dissolves without producing any ions, so its solution cannot conduct electricity and the bulb would not glow. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between electrolyte strength, ion count per formula unit, and solution conductivity to rank four equal-concentration solutions.$px$ WHERE id = '58f52d27-a8b0-42be-8881-887bfea39745';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Nearly every amino acid found in proteins is an L-isomer and, when its absolute configuration is assigned using Cahn-Ingold-Prelog (CIP) priority rules, is designated (S) at the \(\alpha\)-carbon. One proteinogenic amino acid, however, is designated (R) despite having the identical L-spatial arrangement of its four substituents. Which amino acid is the exception?$px$ WHERE id = '5982b835-e805-4f47-baf1-5c1282149586';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is A because among the three ketone bodies, acetone is the odd one out: it forms by spontaneous decarboxylation of acetoacetate, has no CoA-reactivation pathway, and is a small, volatile neutral molecule. Because it cannot be funneled back to acetyl-CoA and readily enters the gas phase, it partitions into alveolar air and is exhaled, producing the fruity breath. Acetoacetate and \(\beta\)-hydroxybutyrate, by contrast, are non-volatile acids that peripheral tissues do oxidize for fuel.

(Choice A) is correct: acetone's volatility combined with its lack of any metabolic reactivation route is exactly why it is lost in breath instead of serving as fuel.
(Choice B) is incorrect: acetoacetate is a non-volatile carboxylic acid that tissues actively use as fuel via ketolysis, so it is neither the volatile species nor the one exhaled. This choice misassigns acetone's property to acetoacetate.
(Choice C) is incorrect: \(\beta\)-hydroxybutyrate is a charged, non-volatile acid (the major circulating fuel ketone), so it is retained and oxidized, not exhaled. The reasoning here contradicts its physical properties.
(Choice D) is incorrect: it names acetone correctly but attributes its exhalation to a missing transferase in peripheral tissues; acetone is intrinsically non-metabolizable and volatile, so the loss is due to its own chemistry, not a tissue-enzyme deficiency.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must reason from the distinguishing physical and metabolic properties of acetone to why it, unlike the other two ketone bodies, is exhaled rather than burned.$px$ WHERE id = '59c408b0-83ef-45bb-8115-6a9cf039a4ff';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$In cardiac muscle, glycolytic reducing equivalents enter the mitochondrion via the malate-aspartate shuttle, which regenerates NADH inside the matrix. A researcher notes that each cytosolic NADH handled this way ultimately supports about 2.5 ATP, the same as a matrix NADH generated by the citric acid cycle. Why does routing the electrons through this shuttle preserve the full ~2.5 ATP yield per cytosolic NADH?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is A because the ATP yield tracks how many proton-pumping complexes the electrons pass through. The malate-aspartate shuttle regenerates \(\text{NADH}\) in the matrix, and matrix \(\text{NADH}\) is oxidized by Complex I; its electrons therefore move through Complex I, then III, then IV (all three pumping complexes), building the full proton-motive force that supports about 2.5 ATP.

(Choice A) is correct: entry at Complex I means the electrons drive proton pumping at I, III, and IV, which is why the per-\(\text{NADH}\) yield equals that of a citric-acid-cycle \(\text{NADH}\).

(Choice B) is incorrect: the shuttle moves reducing equivalents, not intact ATP; ATP is synthesized later at ATP synthase from the proton gradient, not ferried across by the shuttle.

(Choice C) is incorrect: matrix \(\text{NADH}\) is oxidized AT Complex I, not downstream of it; electrons that skip Complex I (entering at coenzyme Q) pump fewer protons and give a lower yield, the opposite of what preserves 2.5 ATP.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Complex II does not pump protons at all, so coupling to Complex II could only lower the yield, and the shuttle does not feed Complex II in any case.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must connect the entry point of the electrons to the number of pumping complexes traversed rather than recall a number.$px$ WHERE id = '59c9c606-71d8-421c-87b0-0d4fdee82c9f';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Acetyl-CoA carboxylase spends ATP to attach a molecule of \(\text{CO}_2\) onto acetyl-CoA, forming the three-carbon malonyl-CoA. Fatty-acid synthase then uses malonyl-CoA, not free acetyl-CoA, as the two-carbon donor in each condensation. During that condensation, the \(\text{CO}_2\) that was just added is released again, and its departure makes the new carbon-carbon bond form readily. Only 2 of malonyl-CoA's 3 carbons end up in the fatty acid. Which statement best explains the role of that added-then-released \(\text{CO}_2\)?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is D because the stem describes a bookkeeping cycle: ACC spends ATP to put \(\text{CO}_2\) on acetyl-CoA (making malonyl-CoA), and that same \(\text{CO}_2\) leaves during the condensation, its departure making the new C-C bond form readily. Because the \(\text{CO}_2\) is added and then removed, only 2 of malonyl-CoA's 3 carbons are incorporated; the \(\text{CO}_2\) acts as a temporary activating handle, not as a building block. This is exactly why malonyl-CoA, rather than acetyl-CoA itself, serves as the two-carbon donor: the added carboxyl makes the subsequent condensation thermodynamically favorable.

(Choice D) is correct: the \(\text{CO}_2\) primes the carbon and is released to drive the condensation, so it never joins the chain.

(Choice A) is incorrect: the \(\text{CO}_2\) leaves during condensation, so it cannot become a permanent carbon of palmitate; the carboxyl carbon of the finished chain comes from the primer, not from the departing \(\text{CO}_2\).

(Choice B) is incorrect: it directly contradicts the stem. Only 2 of malonyl-CoA's 3 carbons are incorporated, because the third (the added \(\text{CO}_2\)) is released each cycle.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the reduction steps draw electrons from NADPH; \(\text{CO}_2\) is not an electron donor, and its role here is activation-and-release, not supplying reducing equivalents.

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must track the fate of the carbons and reason about why the added \(\text{CO}_2\) is not incorporated.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"It stays behind as the terminal carboxyl carbon of the finished chain, which is why palmitate ends in a \\(-\\text{COOH}\\) group rather than a methyl group","label":"A"},{"text":"It is retained in the growing chain as one of the two carbons added each cycle, so over many cycles malonyl-CoA ends up contributing all three of its carbons","label":"B"},{"text":"It supplies the electrons used in the reduction steps of each cycle, coupling the carboxylation event directly to the reductive part of the pathway","label":"C"},{"text":"It is a temporary activating handle: adding it primes the carbon, and its loss during condensation drives the C-C bond formation, so it is not incorporated","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '59e2880b-b992-4aa6-bafe-fa61b82d8f2b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is A because \(K_m\) is the substrate concentration at which an enzyme reaches half of \(V_{max}\), and it behaves as an "effective" dissociation constant for the enzyme-substrate pair: \(K_m\) can be treated as an effective (though not literal) \(K_D\) for the enzyme and its substrate. Because a dissociation constant is an inverse measure of binding strength (lower \(K_D\) values indicate tighter binding), the enzyme with the smaller \(K_m\) (E1, \(0.1\ \text{mM}\)) reaches half-saturation at a much lower substrate concentration and therefore binds the substrate more tightly than E2 (\(5\ \text{mM}\)). \(V_{max}\) is governed by a different relationship, \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), so it reflects catalytic turnover and enzyme amount, not how tightly the substrate is bound. (Choice A) is correct: E1's lower \(K_m\) means it is half-saturated at a lower \([S]\), the hallmark of higher substrate affinity (an "effective" lower \(K_D\)). (Choice B) is wrong: E2's higher \(V_{max}\) reflects faster turnover (\(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\)), which measures catalytic throughput, not binding affinity; a faster enzyme can still bind its substrate weakly. (Choice C) is wrong: a higher \(K_m\) signals weaker, not stronger, binding (it takes more substrate to reach half-saturation), so E2 has the lower affinity. That reverses the correct relationship. (Choice D) is wrong: \(V_{max}\) is set by \(k_{cat}[E]_0\) and does not describe how long substrate is retained or how tightly it binds, so a lower \(V_{max}\) does not imply higher affinity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of \(K_m\) as an effective dissociation constant, distinguish it from \(V_{max}\) (\(k_{cat}[E]_0\)), and reason from two enzymes' kinetic parameters to compare their substrate affinities.$px$ WHERE id = '5a4ec530-3bd3-46a6-a746-34964421e87f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is B because meiosis II separates the two sister chromatids of each chromosome without removing the single chromosome set the cell already possesses, so ploidy stays at n and the division is equational rather than reductional. The reduction in ploidy from two sets to one occurs earlier, when homologous chromosomes separate in meiosis I: "the ploidy level...has been reduced from two to one. For this reason, meiosis I is referred to as a reductional division." Meiosis II instead resembles mitosis: "During anaphase II, as in mitotic anaphase, the kinetochores divide and one sister chromatid (now referred to as a chromosome) is pulled to one pole while the other sister chromatid is pulled to the other pole." The text also states plainly that "Meiosis II is not a reduction division because although there are fewer copies of the genome in the resulting cells, there is still one set of chromosomes, as there was at the end of meiosis I." (Choice A) is wrong because homologous chromosomes separate in meiosis I, not meiosis II; assigning the meiosis-I event to meiosis II misidentifies which division is reductional. (Choice B) is correct because separating sister chromatids leaves the single chromosome set intact, keeping the cell haploid and making meiosis II equational. (Choice C) is wrong because no second drop in ploidy occurs. After meiosis II "there is still one set of chromosomes," so the cell does not fall below n. (Choice D) is wrong because the synaptonemal complex forms only during prophase I to mediate crossing over and breaks down before meiosis II, so no new crossover occurs in meiosis II. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it asks the student to recall the defined distinction between the reductional division (meiosis I) and the equational division (meiosis II) and apply it to a stated cellular event.$px$ WHERE id = '5ba1fe6a-c1f1-4fb8-aaa2-1e5ec9e7c43e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers.' During neurulation the neural tube, the precursor of the brain and spinal cord, arises from dorsal ectoderm, but that ectoderm does not fold on its own: the notochord, a mesodermal rod lying beneath it, releases signals that instruct the overlying cells to become the neural plate. In vertebrates the notochord induces the development of the neural tube, and this dorsal hollow nerve cord derives from ectoderm that rolls into a hollow tube during development. (Choice A) is correct: because the notochord provides the inductive signal that converts overlying ectoderm into the neural plate, removing it before induction leaves the ectoderm without its cue, so that stretch of ectoderm stays epidermal and no neural tube forms in the operated region. (Choice B) is incorrect: loss of the inducer produces a deficiency, not a compensatory duplication; a second, ectopic neural tube would instead be expected from adding an extra notochord, not from removing one. (Choice C) is incorrect: the ectoderm does not autonomously roll into a neural tube on a fixed schedule. Neurulation depends on the notochord's induction, so without that signal the folding does not occur. (Choice D) is incorrect: the neural tube is an ectodermal derivative, and the endoderm (which forms the digestive and respiratory linings) is neither the source of the neural tube nor a substitute inducer for the notochord. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of notochord-mediated neural induction to predict the result of an experimental perturbation the stem does not describe.$px$ WHERE id = '5c59d324-ebf4-40f5-8219-20f9684add35';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The diagnostic here is the pair of kinetic observations: a very large excess of substrate fully restores the original maximum velocity (\(V_M\) unchanged), while a higher substrate concentration is now needed to reach half-maximal rate (apparent \(K_M\) increased). With a competitive inhibitor, driving substrate concentration toward infinity converts all enzyme to the enzyme-substrate complex, so \(V_M\) is not changed; but the inhibitor shifting the E + S equilibrium leftward makes it appear that the affinity of E for S has decreased, raising the apparent \(K_M\). That signature (\(V_M\) recoverable by excess substrate, \(K_M\) up) points to a molecule that occupies the active site itself, since only there can added substrate directly displace it. (Choice A) is correct: a molecule that competes for the active site is outcompeted when substrate is in large excess, restoring \(V_M\), and it raises the apparent \(K_M\) exactly as the data show, because substrate and inhibitor both bind the same site on the enzyme and compete for the active site. (Choice B) is incorrect: a molecule that binds a separate site and lowers \(V_M\) in a way excess substrate cannot overcome describes noncompetitive inhibition, in which the inhibitor does not enter the pocket where the substrate binds; that would leave \(V_M\) depressed, contradicting the observed full recovery of maximum velocity. (Choice C) is incorrect: a molecule binding only the enzyme-substrate complex describes uncompetitive inhibition, where the inhibitor binds only to ES and not to free enzyme, so that both \(V_M\) and \(K_M\) decrease. Here, though, \(V_M\) is fully restored and \(K_M\) rises, the opposite pattern. (Choice D) is incorrect: a molecule binding free enzyme and the ES complex with equal affinity and leaving the half-maximal substrate concentration unchanged describes pure noncompetitive inhibition, in which \(K_M\) remains unchanged while \(V_M\) falls; the data instead show \(V_M\) recoverable and \(K_M\) increased. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the kinetic signature (full recovery of \(V_M\) at high substrate with an increased apparent \(K_M\)) to infer that the molecule binds the active site and competes with substrate.$px$ WHERE id = '5c7ad5d0-aa16-4274-ae07-a612b3192178';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Solid ammonium chloride, \(\text{NH}_4\text{Cl}\), is the salt of the strong acid HCl and the weak base \(\text{NH}_3\). It is dissolved in pure water to give a \(0.10\,\text{M}\) solution. Predict whether the resulting solution is acidic, basic, or neutral, and identify the species responsible.$px$ WHERE id = '5cacabec-8128-4db8-bdb7-0d474aac4a00';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A galvanic cell is initially set up under standard conditions, so its potential equals the standard cell potential, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\). The cell is then altered so that its reaction quotient becomes less than one (\(Q < 1\)), for example by raising the concentration of a reactant ion or lowering the concentration of a product ion. The cell continues to operate at 298 K, where the Nernst equation takes the form \(E_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.0592}{n}\log Q\). Once \(Q < 1\), how does the new cell potential \(E_{\text{cell}}\) compare to the standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\)?$px$ WHERE id = '5cef5a9f-bd93-4593-9aec-a2b57d503ed1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because sustained stress keeps cortisol chronically elevated, and cortisol's job is to mobilize fuel and defend blood glucose. It degrades muscle protein to release amino acids, stimulates adipose lipolysis, channels those amino acids into hepatic gluconeogenesis, and inhibits glucose uptake by peripheral tissues. Together these push blood glucose up while raising circulating amino acids and fatty acids.

(Choice D) is correct: it captures all four coordinated actions (proteolysis, lipolysis, hepatic gluconeogenesis, and suppressed peripheral uptake) that jointly elevate blood glucose.
(Choice A) is incorrect: this describes an insulin-like, anabolic profile (protein synthesis, fat storage, lower glucose), the opposite of cortisol's catabolic, glucose-raising effect.
(Choice B) is incorrect: cortisol does not spare fat; it increases lipolysis, so plasma fatty acids rise rather than fall.
(Choice C) is incorrect: cortisol inhibits peripheral glucose uptake rather than enhancing it, which is one reason blood glucose climbs.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by asking you to integrate cortisol's separate tissue-level actions into a single net metabolic prediction.$px$ WHERE id = '5d1e411e-7542-49e2-aee7-3d20a43f36d0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is A because allopatric speciation begins when a geographic discontinuity blocks the free movement of alleles between populations. Within a continuous population, gene flow, the movement of alleles across a species' range, is relatively free. When populations become geographically discontinuous, this free flow of alleles is prevented, and when that separation lasts for a period of time, the two populations are able to evolve along different trajectories, so that their allele frequencies at numerous genetic loci gradually become increasingly different as new alleles independently arise by mutation in each population. The barrier itself does not create mutations or impose a reproductive barrier; it simply stops gene flow, which then permits independent divergence. (Choice A) is correct: halting gene flow is the direct effect of the geographic barrier, and it is the loss of this free flow of alleles that allows the two isolated populations to diverge independently. (Choice B) is wrong: mutations arise independently in each population over time but are random events, not generated on demand by the barrier; the barrier does not produce adaptive mutations. (Choice C) is wrong: polyploidy is a chromosomal error that drives sympatric speciation within the same space, not the allopatric mechanism caused by a geographic barrier. (Choice D) is wrong: a postzygotic barrier is a reproductive isolation mechanism that occurs after zygote formation, an eventual outcome of prolonged divergence rather than the direct, immediate effect of the river splitting the population. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that allopatric speciation is initiated by geographic separation halting gene flow, allowing isolated populations to diverge along independent trajectories.$px$ WHERE id = '5d226eab-9324-4995-8fb8-b1ad9d95bce0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because the anomeric carbon of cyclic D-glucose bears a hemiacetal, a carbon holding both an \(-OR\) (the ring oxygen) and an \(-OH\) group. When acid protonates the anomeric hydroxyl, that \(-OH\) leaves as water, generating a resonance-stabilized oxocarbenium ion; the incoming methanol oxygen then adds to the anomeric carbon. The carbon now carries two \(-OR\) groups (the ring oxygen and the new \(-OCH_3\)), which is by definition an acetal. This carbon-\(O\)-methanol acetal linkage is a glycosidic bond, and the sugar-\(OCH_3\) product is a glycoside (methyl glucoside). Because the anomeric \(-OH\) departs as \(H_2O\) while methanol supplies the new \(-OR\), exactly one molecule of water is released, making this a condensation.

(Choice A) is incorrect: A hemiacetal has one \(-OR\) and one \(-OH\) on the same carbon; once the second alcohol adds and water leaves, the carbon has two \(-OR\) groups and is no longer a hemiacetal but a full acetal, so the linkage cannot remain a hemiacetal.

(Choice B) is correct: adding a second alcohol to the anomeric hemiacetal, with loss of water, produces an acetal joined by a glycosidic bond.

(Choice C) is incorrect: An ester forms between a carboxylic acid (or acyl group) and an alcohol; glucose has no carboxyl group at its anomeric carbon, so reaction with an alcohol at that carbon yields an acetal, not an ester, even though both reactions release water.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Simply fusing two intact hydroxyl oxygens into an ether would conserve all atoms and release nothing, but that misreads the mechanism: the anomeric hydroxyl is expelled as water, and the product carbon bears two \(-OR\) groups (an acetal), not a single \(-O-\) ether bridge.

This question requires a Skill 2 process: applying the general hemiacetal-plus-alcohol-to-acetal transformation to the specific case of a monosaccharide's anomeric carbon and predicting both the product's bond type and the released byproduct.$px$ WHERE id = '5d5a6aa6-5f14-4a42-8aa9-1ab6d3b8433e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' The cytoskeleton has three fiber types with distinct jobs: microfilaments (actin) drive movement and the contractile ring of cytokinesis, microtubules (tubulin) form tracks for vesicle transport and pull chromosomes apart, and intermediate filaments are the diverse, purely structural group that bear tension, thereby maintaining the cell's shape and anchoring the nucleus and other organelles in place. Keratin is a classic example of an intermediate-filament protein, one that strengthens hair, nails, and the epidermis of the skin. (Choice A) is correct: a keratin defect removes an intermediate filament, and the phenotype is a purely mechanical one, with cells that tear when strained but still move, divide, and traffic vesicles. That pattern matches the tension-bearing structural role that intermediate filaments alone provide. (Choice B) is incorrect: long-range vesicle transport runs on microtubules, which provide a track along which vesicles move through the cell; the stem states vesicle shuttling is still normal, so this microtubule function is intact, and keratin is not a tubulin polymer. (Choice C) is incorrect: cytokinesis is carried out by an actin contractile ring, one of the motions actin powers, such as cell division; the cells still divide normally, so this microfilament role is preserved. (Choice D) is incorrect: crawling depends on microfilaments that depolymerize (disassemble) and reform quickly, enabling a cell to change its shape and move, and the cells still migrate across the dish; moreover, intermediate filaments have no role in cell movement, so this dynamic motility function was never theirs to lose. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the functional profile of each cytoskeletal fiber to a phenotype, recognizing that a defect causing mechanical fragility while leaving movement, division, and transport intact points to the purely structural, tension-bearing intermediate filaments, even though the stem never names that class.$px$ WHERE id = '5e881100-8821-45b7-9cd4-22a4cd759d9a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is A because map distance in centimorgans corresponds directly to recombination frequency expressed as a percentage: a \(0.01\) (i.e., \(1\%\)) recombination frequency equals \(1\ \text{cM}\). A recombination frequency of \(8\%\) therefore equals \(8 \times 1\ \text{cM} = 8\ \text{cM}\). Map distances in centimorgans correspond to recombination frequencies expressed in percent; for example, genes recombining in \(17\%\) of offspring lie \(17\ \text{cM}\) apart. (Choice A) is correct: converting \(8\%\) recombination directly to \(8\ \text{cM}\) applies the \(1\%{=}1\ \text{cM}\) relationship exactly. (Choice B) \(4\ \text{cM}\) results from incorrectly halving the recombination frequency, as if only one of the two reciprocal recombinant classes counted toward the distance; the full recombinant frequency defines the distance. (Choice C) \(0.08\ \text{cM}\) confuses the decimal form of the frequency (\(0.08\)) with the percentage form, an error of \(100\times\); the centimorgan tracks the percentage, not the decimal fraction. (Choice D) \(16\ \text{cM}\) results from doubling the recombination frequency, which has no basis. Each \(1\%\) maps to exactly \(1\ \text{cM}\), not \(2\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between recombination frequency and centimorgan distance to compute a genetic map distance from cross data.$px$ WHERE id = '5e88ffa3-00d2-4ff1-b8c1-14a86014f79b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is A because kinesin's directed stepping along a microtubule is powered by ATP hydrolysis, so a motor that can bind but not hydrolyze ATP cannot undergo the conformational changes needed to move and stalls. (Choice A) is correct: force generation by these motor proteins involves ATP hydrolysis coupled to allosteric shifts in the orientation of the motor domains, allowing the protein to step progressively down the microtubule, so blocking hydrolysis abolishes directed movement even though ATP is bound. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that nucleotide binding alone is sufficient for motility, but hydrolysis of ATP, not merely binding, drives the allosteric shifts that produce stepping. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that kinesin's directionality can flip; kinesin moves toward the plus end of microtubules, and directionality is a fixed structural property, not something a hydrolysis-blocking mutation reverses. (Choice D) is incorrect: this confuses the role of microtubule polarity, which determines the direction of travel, with the source of energy; motion requires ATP hydrolysis, so polarity cannot substitute as an energy source. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that kinesin motor proteins convert the chemical energy of ATP hydrolysis into directed, plus-end movement along microtubules.$px$ WHERE id = '5ec63e8c-f8ee-45dd-94a3-26f20e78564e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).' The endometrium consists of two layers: the stratum basalis, which lies adjacent to the myometrium and does not shed during menses, and the overlying stratum functionalis, which thickens under estrogen and progesterone, provides the implantation site, and is the only layer lost at menstruation. Because the functionalis is shed each cycle, it must be rebuilt from the retained stratum basalis; once menstrual flow ceases, rising estrogen stimulates the lining to proliferate again from this basal layer. The "deepest layer of the endometrium" destroyed by scarring is therefore the stratum basalis, the regenerative source. (Choice B) is correct: with the stratum basalis destroyed, the scarred region loses the base from which the functionalis regrows, so after each menstrual shedding that region cannot rebuild its lining and remains persistently thin. (Choice A) is incorrect: it is the stratum functionalis, not the basalis, that sheds at menstruation; destroying the deep basalis removes the regenerative base rather than blocking shedding, so a normally thickened lining that simply fails to shed is not the expected outcome. (Choice C) is incorrect: shedding is triggered by the cyclic fall in progesterone acting on the functionalis, not by the presence of the basalis, so losing the basalis would not convert normal cyclic shedding into continuous shedding. (Choice D) is incorrect: excessive thickening would require an intact functionalis responding to hormones, but the scarred region has lost its regenerative base, so it cannot build up a thick lining at all, let alone an excessive one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the two-layer structure of the endometrium to a novel clinical scenario, identifying the destroyed deep layer as the non-shedding stratum basalis and reasoning that regeneration of the functionalis depends on it.$px$ WHERE id = '5f223024-0f2b-4379-a6cb-bd7e43eae4cc';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher characterizing distinctive side-chain features of individual amino acids drafts the following pairings. Consider which of the residue-feature pairings are correct.

I. Tryptophan carries a bicyclic indole ring that contains a nitrogen atom.
II. Tyrosine bears a phenol (a hydroxyl bonded directly to the aromatic ring) that raises its reactivity and solubility relative to phenylalanine.
III. Methionine presents a free thiol \(-\text{SH}\) side chain.$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because statements I and II describe genuine distinctive features while statement III misassigns a thiol to the wrong sulfur-containing residue. Tryptophan's side chain is an indole ring whose nitrogen sits in a bicyclic aromatic system, and tyrosine's phenol, a hydroxyl bonded directly to the aromatic ring, is exactly what increases its reactivity and solubility compared with phenylalanine. Methionine's sulfur is held in a thioether linkage that cannot form a permanent dipole and retains low solubility; the free thiol \(-\text{SH}\) (sulfhydryl) belongs to cysteine instead, so statement III is false. The pairings that hold are therefore I and II only. (Choice A) III only selects the single false statement, treating methionine as if it carried cysteine's thiol while discarding both true pairings. (Choice B) I and III only keeps the correct tryptophan pairing but again accepts the wrong-sulfur thiol for methionine. (Choice C) II and III only keeps the correct tyrosine pairing but still accepts the false methionine-thiol pairing while dropping the true tryptophan feature. (Choice D) is correct: I and II are the residue-feature pairings that hold, and III is rejected because methionine's sulfur is a thioether, not a free thiol. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the specific side-chain structures of tryptophan, tyrosine, phenylalanine, methionine, and cysteine and apply them to judge each pairing.$px$ WHERE id = '5f380e0e-adef-4635-a7c4-d08e8245c318';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Two zinc-carbon dry cells of the same design, a large D cell and a small AAA cell, are each connected to an identical small motor. A voltmeter reads about 1.5 V across each cell, yet the D cell keeps its motor running many times longer than the AAA cell before the motor stops. Which statement best accounts for both observations?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC).' The voltage (cell potential) of a battery is fixed by the standard reduction potentials of the two half-reactions in its redox couple, so it does not depend on how large the cell is; all sizes of a given dry cell (AAA, AA, C, D) use the same components and therefore exhibit the same voltage of about 1.5 V. What changes with size is the amount of reactant: a larger cell packs in more of the redox reactants and can therefore transfer correspondingly more total charge before the reaction is exhausted. (Choice A) is correct: because both cells run the same zinc/manganese(IV) oxide chemistry, both produce the same ~1.5 V regardless of size, and the D cell outlasts the AAA cell because its greater mass of reactant lets it deliver more total charge. (Choice B) is incorrect: cell voltage is an intensive property set by the redox couple and does not rise when current is drawn (if anything, real cells sag slightly under load), so a larger cell does not operate at a higher voltage. (Choice C) is incorrect: adding more of the same reactant or using larger electrodes increases capacity, not cell potential; the potential is fixed by the identity of the half-reactions, which is why the voltmeter reads the same ~1.5 V for both cells. (Choice D) is incorrect: the two cells do not hold the same total charge. Capacity scales with the amount of reactant, so the physically larger D cell stores far more charge. Internal resistance affects how much current a cell can supply, not how long it lasts on the same small load. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction that battery voltage is set by the redox chemistry (intensive) while total charge capacity is set by the quantity of reactant (extensive) to explain equal voltage but unequal lifetime.$px$ WHERE id = '5f6a316b-b532-4b98-b4f1-14485ff7988a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the blood-brain barrier readily permits dissolved CO₂ to diffuse across into the cerebrospinal fluid, where it forms carbonic acid and lowers CSF pH almost immediately, directly stimulating the central chemoreceptors; in contrast, the barrier is relatively impermeable to charged H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so a metabolic acid load in the arterial blood cannot rapidly alter CSF pH and reaches the central receptors only slowly. (Choice A) is correct: it correctly pairs high CO₂ permeability with low H⁺/HCO₃⁻ permeability, which is exactly why respiratory (CO₂) changes act fast centrally while metabolic acids act slowly. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the true permeabilities. CO₂ crosses readily and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ do not, so this would predict the opposite (fast metabolic, slow CO₂) response. (Choice C) is incorrect: the difference is a transport phenomenon at the barrier, not a matter of plasma buffering; if the barrier were equally permeable to all three, metabolic acid would reach the receptors as fast as CO₂. (Choice D) is incorrect: the barrier does not actively pump H⁺ into the CSF to amplify metabolic signals; H⁺ movement is restricted, which is why the metabolic response is slow, not amplified. Skill: This question tests the ability to reason from a structural property (selective barrier permeability) to a functional physiological consequence (differential speed of central chemoreceptor stimulation).$px$ WHERE id = '5f6f22f3-1986-4a99-8e7f-946b05612569';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A solution of free histidine is buffered so that the imidazole side chain (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 6.0\)) sits exactly at its half-equivalence point, where it is 50% protonated. Using histidine's other group \(\text{pK}_a\) values (\(\alpha\)-carboxyl \(\approx 1.8\) and \(\alpha\)-amino \(\approx 9.2\)), what average net charge does histidine most likely carry under these conditions?$px$ WHERE id = '5f96036d-c259-4ded-9c8f-7f2f837ed133';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because the peroxisome is the organelle that carries out the oxidation reactions that begin breaking down fatty acids, so it initiates the chain-shortening of the very-long-chain \(\text{C}_{26}\) substrate. Peroxisomes carry out oxidation reactions that break down fatty acids and amino acids, making the peroxisome, not the mitochondrion, the fatty-acid-oxidizing organelle; the very-long-chain \(\text{C}_{26}\) chain is first shortened in the peroxisome, after which the shortened product is handed off for completion of oxidation in the mitochondrion. Because the question asks where breakdown of the \(\text{C}_{26}\) chain BEGINS, the initiating organelle is the peroxisome. The mitochondrion is the powerhouse that makes ATP by cellular respiration using oxygen, a downstream and energy-yielding role, not the initiating one for this oversized substrate. (Choice A) The mitochondrion does generate ATP by cellular respiration, but ATP production is not the step that initiates breakdown of an oversized \(\text{C}_{26}\) chain; naming the mitochondrion here is an adjacent-fact trap that substitutes the organelle's energy role for the initiating oxidative-shortening role that the peroxisome performs. (Choice B) This reverses the actual division of labor: it casts the mitochondrion as the initiator of oxidation for fatty acids of all chain lengths, whereas the fatty-acid-oxidation function belongs to peroxisomes. The mitochondrion's role is making ATP via respiration, so claiming it begins oxidation of the very-long-chain substrate inverts the peroxisome-to-mitochondrion hand-off. (Choice C) Lysosomes are not fatty-acid-oxidizing organelles; the structure responsible for the oxidation reactions that break down fatty acids is the peroxisome. Invoking acid-hydrolase digestion misapplies a different organelle's degradative chemistry to the oxidative chain-shortening at issue, making this a misconception trap. (Choice D) Correct. Peroxisomes carry out oxidation reactions that break down fatty acids and amino acids; these single-membrane organelles begin oxidatively shortening the very-long-chain \(\text{C}_{26}\) fatty acid before the truncated chain is passed to the mitochondrion, so the peroxisome is the initiating organelle. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the assignment of fatty-acid oxidation to peroxisomes to a specific clinical scenario and reason out which organelle BEGINS breakdown of an oversized substrate rather than simply recalling each organelle's headline function.$px$ WHERE id = '5fd8a53d-e16c-4fe7-bf97-36d997d34327';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$During the alkaline processing of a glucose solution, the open-chain sugar transiently interconverts between a form in which C-2 carries a carbonyl group and a form in which the bond between C-1 and C-2 is a carbon-carbon double bond bearing a hydroxyl substituent (an "-ene-diol" arrangement). At equilibrium, which form of this two-carbon fragment predominates, and for what reason?

I. The form with the C=C bearing the hydroxyl group predominates
II. The form with the C-2 carbonyl predominates
III. The two forms are present in essentially equal amounts
IV. The favored form is the one lower in free energy$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because the two interconverting forms described are the keto and enol tautomers of the sugar fragment. The C-2 carbonyl form is the keto tautomer, and the form with a double bond between C-1 and C-2 bearing a hydroxyl group is the enol tautomer. A carbon-oxygen double bond (the carbonyl) is stronger and more stable than the combination of a carbon-carbon double bond plus an O-H bond found in the enol, so the keto tautomer sits lower in free energy. At equilibrium the lower-energy species predominates, so both statement II (the carbonyl form predominates) and statement IV (the favored form is the one lower in free energy) are correct, and together they identify the keto form as favored.

(Choice A) is incorrect: This selects the enol form (I) and offers a spurious reason: a carbon-carbon \(\pi\) bond does not stabilize an isolated system better than a carbon-oxygen \(\pi\) bond. The stronger, more polarized C=O of the carbonyl makes the keto tautomer the more stable one, so the enol does not predominate.

(Choice B) is incorrect: This reflects the misconception that because tautomers differ "only" by the position of a proton and a double bond, they must be isoenergetic. Tautomers are distinct constitutional isomers with different bonding, and the keto and enol forms differ substantially in free energy; they are not present in equal amounts.

(Choice D) is correct: Statement II correctly names the carbonyl (keto) form as predominant, and statement IV correctly states that the thermodynamically favored tautomer is the one of lower free energy; the keto form satisfies both.

(Choice C) is incorrect: This pairs the correct thermodynamic principle (IV, that the lower-energy form is favored) with the wrong identification of that form. It labels the enol (double bond plus hydroxyl) as lower in energy, but the carbonyl form is actually lower in free energy, so I is incorrect here.

This is a Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) question because it requires recalling the relative stability of keto versus enol tautomers and applying the definition that the tautomer favored at equilibrium is the one lower in free energy.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"I, because a carbon-carbon double bond delocalizes electron density more effectively than a carbon-oxygen double bond","label":"A"},{"text":"III, because the two forms are related by simply shifting a proton and a double bond, so they are equal in energy","label":"B"},{"text":"I and IV, because the double-bond-plus-hydroxyl form lies lower in free energy than the carbonyl-containing form","label":"C"},{"text":"II and IV, because the carbonyl-containing form lies lower in free energy than the double-bond-plus-hydroxyl form","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '60240917-5d0f-49e3-a85e-b08444d9ff87';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is C because \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{ATP}\) are reciprocally related: a rising \(\text{ADP}:\text{ATP}\) ratio means \(\text{ATP}\) is being consumed faster than it is replaced, which is the hallmark of an energy deficit (low energy charge). More \(\text{ADP}\) both signals demand and supplies \(\text{ATP}\) synthase with its substrate, so oxidative phosphorylation should speed up to restore \(\text{ATP}\).

(Choice C) is correct: a high \(\text{ADP}:\text{ATP}\) ratio marks an energy shortfall and drives oxphos faster to refill the \(\text{ATP}\) pool.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the meaning of the ratio. A high \(\text{ADP}:\text{ATP}\) ratio reflects deficit, not surplus, so the correct response is to speed up, not slow down.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly reads the ratio as a deficit but then draws the wrong response; a deficit calls for making more \(\text{ATP}\), so oxphos speeds up rather than slowing.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it pairs a wrong reading (surplus) with the right direction (speed up) only by coincidence; a surplus would in fact slow oxphos, so the reasoning is internally inconsistent with the biology.

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must reason from the \(\text{ADP}:\text{ATP}\) ratio to the energy-state signal it represents and the appropriate change in oxphos rate.$px$ WHERE id = '604729bd-4496-41c0-8a76-be60d21be688';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Under standard conditions, the overall reaction in a hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell, \(2\,\text{H}_2(g) + \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}(l)\), has a standard Gibbs free energy change of \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -474\ \text{kJ/mol}\) and transfers \(n = 4\) moles of electrons per mole of reaction as written. Using \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), what is the standard cell potential, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), for this cell?$px$ WHERE id = '60cdcd41-5625-4e00-a70f-e228923b61ab';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because dilating the mucosal capillary beds of the nasal passages and trachea increases warm blood flow just beneath the airway surface, and the large surface area of moving air lets that heat transfer to the airstream (and drive evaporation of mucosal water), so more heat is carried out of the body with each exhalation. (Choice A) is correct: vasodilation brings more warm blood to the airway surface, maximizing heat loss to inspired/expired air and supporting evaporative cooling. That is the appropriate response to a heat load. (Choice B) is incorrect: vasoconstriction reduces surface blood flow, which conserves heat rather than dissipating it; that is the response to cold, not heat. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly notes that evaporation cools, but constriction reduces the warm blood and moisture delivered to the surface, so it impairs rather than drives evaporative cooling. (Choice D) is incorrect: it pairs the correct vascular change (dilation) with the wrong outcome. Dilation delivers blood outward toward the surface to lose heat, not inward to conserve it. This question tests the skill of reasoning about how a physiological mechanism responds to a described environmental challenge.$px$ WHERE id = '60f14206-a5dd-46bb-9725-8d42c053d3b2';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is D because hydration is the second of the four \(\beta\)-oxidation steps, so the intermediate described is a 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA about to enter step 3: the 3-hydroxyl (an alcohol) is oxidized to a 3-keto group. Oxidizing a C-OH to a C=O is a large-enough energy drop that \(\text{NAD}^+\) serves as the acceptor, generating \(\text{NADH}\).

(Choice D) is correct: after hydration, 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase oxidizes the alcohol to a ketone and passes the electrons to \(\text{NAD}^+\), yielding \(\text{NADH}\).
(Choice A) is incorrect: introducing the \(trans\)-\(\Delta 2\) double bond with \(\text{FAD}\) is step 1, which already happened before the hydration described in the stem. It is not the next step.
(Choice B) is incorrect: thiolysis is step 4 and does release acetyl-CoA, but it does not reduce a cofactor and it does not immediately follow hydration; the oxidation of the alcohol comes first.
(Choice C) is incorrect: the alcohol→ketone oxidation does happen next, but it uses \(\text{NAD}^+\), not \(\text{FAD}\); \(\text{FAD}\) is the acceptor only for the earlier C-C→C=C step.

By locating the described intermediate within the ordered sequence and predicting the next reaction and its cofactor, this item assesses Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific principles).$px$ WHERE id = '611cc4d6-8082-4a1f-ba81-a3f83435d281';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Volunteers kept in constant dim light develop weakened daily fluctuations in sleepiness, and their blood level of one hormone stops rising each evening. That rise normally coincides with the onset of drowsiness. Which hormone shows this evening-linked pattern?$px$ WHERE id = '61fdcd4a-e52d-4b3b-ae7e-25fa15417a08';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).' In the testis, LH binds to receptors on the interstitial (Leydig) cells and upregulates their production of testosterone, while the anterior pituitary's release of LH is held in check by negative feedback: rising testosterone binds androgen receptors on the hypothalamus and pituitary to suppress GnRH and LH, and falling testosterone releases that brake so more LH is secreted. If the LH receptor cannot respond, Leydig cells receive no signal, so testosterone synthesis falls; the resulting low testosterone removes feedback inhibition, so the intact pituitary secretes more LH. (Choice A) is correct: with dead LH receptors the Leydig cells cannot make testosterone, so testosterone falls, and the loss of testosterone-mediated negative feedback drives the hypothalamic-pituitary axis to secrete more LH. (Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly predicts low testosterone but ignores negative feedback. Low testosterone disinhibits the hypothalamus and pituitary, so LH rises rather than falls (a decreased-LH pattern would instead point to a hypothalamic or pituitary defect). (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the primary effect, since a nonfunctional LH receptor cannot stimulate Leydig cells to raise testosterone; without high testosterone there is also no feedback signal to lower LH. (Choice D) is incorrect: because the defect lies in the Leydig cells' own LH receptor, testosterone production cannot be maintained at normal levels, so neither hormone stays normal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the LH-to-Leydig-cell signaling step together with the negative-feedback logic of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis to a novel loss-of-function scenario.$px$ WHERE id = '6219aa88-d5b3-4cc6-95eb-9cde88eb3162';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A patient with a chronic inflammatory condition takes daily high doses of a synthetic steroid drug for several months. The drug binds and activates the same intracellular receptors as the principal stress steroid made by the outer region of the adrenal glands, including the receptors in the hypothalamus and pituitary that sense that steroid's level. After several months, what happens to the anterior pituitary's output of the tropic hormone that normally drives the adrenal cortex, and to the size of the cortex itself?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is C because the synthetic drug occupies the same glucocorticoid receptors as cortisol and therefore mimics its downstream effects, including the negative-feedback signal that suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis; the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary sense an 'adequate' glucocorticoid state and reduce their signaling, so the pituitary's tropic hormone (ACTH) falls. Because ACTH is also the trophic stimulus that maintains the cortex, its chronic withdrawal starves the zona fasciculata of stimulation and the cortex atrophies. (Choice C) is correct: high sustained glucocorticoid-receptor activation drives negative feedback that lowers ACTH, and loss of trophic ACTH support causes the adrenal cortex to shrink. (Choice B) is incorrect: rising tropic hormone with an enlarging cortex describes an ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor (a cause of Cushing's disease), in which the pituitary is the source of excess signal; here the excess glucocorticoid comes from an outside drug, which suppresses ACTH rather than raising it, so this is a partial-truth trap that confuses two different high-cortisol scenarios. (Choice A) is incorrect: it correctly predicts cortical atrophy but reverses the feedback direction, since negative feedback from a glucocorticoid signal lowers ACTH rather than raising it. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly predicts that ACTH falls, but a fall in trophic ACTH cannot enlarge the cortex; without ACTH stimulation the cortex loses mass. Skill 2: this item requires chaining exogenous receptor activation to feedback suppression of ACTH and then to loss of trophic support and cortical atrophy, distinguishing a drug-driven perturbation from a tumor-driven one.$px$ WHERE id = '621f4b3b-c922-49f4-9200-34002b037118';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is A because the drifting rotation in the first trial (mutarotation) requires that the two anomers be able to interconvert in solution. That interconversion is possible only because the cyclic anomeric carbon of glucose is a hemiacetal: it can reversibly open to the open-chain aldehyde form and then reclose, and each reclosure can generate either the α or β anomer. Starting from one pure anomer, this open-and-reclose cycle gradually populates the other anomer until an equilibrium mixture is reached, and because the two anomers have different specific rotations the observed rotation drifts to the equilibrium value. Converting the anomeric \(\text{OH}\) to a methoxy group makes the anomeric carbon a full acetal (a glycoside), not a hemiacetal. An acetal cannot spontaneously ring-open to the aldehyde, so the pathway that allows anomers to interconvert is blocked. With no interconversion, the freshly dissolved single anomer keeps its composition and its rotation stays constant.

(Choice A) is correct: it correctly identifies that mutarotation depends on the anomeric carbon being able to reopen, and that capping it as an acetal removes that intermediate, so no rotation change occurs.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it invents a conformational-locking effect. Even if the methoxy group did favor one chair, a change in preferred chair conformation would not create or prevent the anomer interconversion that mutarotation reports; the deciding factor is whether the anomeric center can open to the open-chain form, not which chair the ring adopts.

(Choice C) is incorrect: glycosides are acetals and are kinetically stable to hydrolysis in neutral water at room temperature (enzymes or strong acid are needed to cleave them on any relevant timescale). The methoxy group is not spontaneously stripped back to a hydroxyl during the measurement, so interconversion is not restored.

(Choice D) is incorrect: dissolving a pure anomer does not instantly yield equal amounts of both forms; interconversion is a gradual approach to equilibrium, and the equilibrium is not 50:50 (β-D-glucopyranose predominates). This choice also ignores that the acetal cannot interconvert at all.

Answering requires reasoning from the mutarotation mechanism to a novel manipulation (recognizing that capping the anomeric carbon eliminates the ring-opening intermediate) rather than recalling a stated definition.$px$ WHERE id = '621f9db6-c3c9-4150-bde5-d8fa93729938';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because fast oxidative (intermediate) fibers produce \(\text{ATP}\) aerobically with abundant mitochondria and resist fatigue, yet lack significant myoglobin, which makes them paler than the red slow oxidative fibers. FO fibers are oxidative because they generate ATP aerobically and possess high numbers of mitochondria, so they do not fatigue quickly; however, they do not contain significant myoglobin, which gives them a lighter color than the red SO fibers. The described profile chains two features: strong oxidative-enzyme staining plus fatigue resistance (an aerobic signature) together with a pale color (low myoglobin). Only the intermediate FO fiber satisfies both. (Choice A) is correct: it is the only fiber type that is genuinely oxidative and fatigue-resistant while still lacking the myoglobin that would deepen its color, exactly the intermediate phenotype between SO and FG fibers. (Choice B) is wrong because slow oxidative fibers also possess high mitochondrial content but additionally contain myoglobin, which gives SO fibers their red color; their deep-red appearance contradicts the paler staining described, so SO fibers are the deep-red postural fibers the biopsy contrasts against, not the pale ones. (Choice C) is wrong because fast glycolytic fibers do not possess substantial numbers of mitochondria or significant amounts of myoglobin; they would indeed be pale, but they would not stain strongly for mitochondrial oxidative enzymes or resist fatigue, since they fatigue quickly. (Choice D) is wrong because smooth muscle is not one of the three skeletal muscle fiber types classified by contraction speed and metabolism (there are three main types of skeletal muscle fibers), and the classification scheme described here applies to skeletal fibers. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining metabolic and myoglobin criteria for each fiber type to an unfamiliar histochemical profile and infer the matching fiber type.$px$ WHERE id = '623b91cb-302d-41ad-b2dc-d8e01803e696';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because a 17-carbon chain cannot be divided evenly into two-carbon pieces. Successive removals of acetyl-CoA leave a final 5-carbon acyl-CoA, whose last cleavage yields one acetyl-CoA and a 3-carbon propionyl-CoA; the odd carbon shows up as this propionyl-CoA rather than as another acetyl-CoA.

(Choice B) is correct: an odd-chain fatty acid yields acetyl-CoA units plus one terminal propionyl-CoA (3 C).
(Choice A) is incorrect: only the final cleavage produces propionyl-CoA; all earlier rounds still release two-carbon acetyl-CoA.
(Choice C) is incorrect: \(\beta\)-oxidation removes two carbons per cut, so the leftover odd unit is a 3-carbon propionyl-CoA, never a 1-carbon 'formyl-CoA.'
(Choice D) is incorrect: an odd-chain fatty acid cannot give only acetyl-CoA. An odd carbon count guarantees a 3-carbon propionyl-CoA remainder.

By reasoning from chain parity to the identity of the end products, this item assesses Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific principles).$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"Every round releases propionyl-CoA instead of acetyl-CoA","label":"A"},{"text":"The final round leaves a 3-carbon propionyl-CoA in addition to the acetyl-CoA units","label":"B"},{"text":"The final round leaves a single unpaired 1-carbon formyl-CoA plus the acetyl-CoA units","label":"C"},{"text":"The products are identical: only acetyl-CoA is formed, just an odd number of molecules","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '62d0141e-8218-4b27-bce1-21d18436cf04';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is A because the only glial cell that myelinates axons in the peripheral nervous system is the Schwann cell, so a defect that strips PNS axons of myelin while sparing the brain and spinal cord must lie in the Schwann-cell lineage. The text states the Schwann cell is the PNS glial cell that "insulate axons with myelin in the periphery," whereas the oligodendrocyte "is the glial cell type that insulates axons in the CNS"; because two distinct cell types build the two myelin pools, impairing one leaves the other intact. This is exactly the observed pattern: bare peripheral axons but fully myelinated central axons, which points to Schwann cells, not the central myelinator. (Choice A) is correct: Schwann cells myelinate the PNS, so their selective loss leaves PNS axons bare while oligodendrocyte-made CNS myelin persists. (Choice B) reverses the locations. Oligodendrocyte impairment would leave CNS axons bare and PNS axons normal, the opposite of what is seen. (Choice C) is wrong because satellite cells merely surround PNS ganglion cell bodies for support and never form myelin, so they cannot be the failed myelinating cell. (Choice D) is wrong because astrocytes are CNS glia that form the blood-brain barrier rather than myelinate peripheral axons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires mapping an observed lesion pattern (PNS-only demyelination) back to the specific cell lineage whose location-restricted function explains the data.$px$ WHERE id = '62d02e02-7a99-4610-8498-0adcaf9c1c80';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because a fall in insulin is the signal of the fasting (post-absorptive) state, and low insulin relieves the suppression of hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL) inside the adipocyte. Active HSL hydrolyzes stored triacylglycerol, mobilizing fatty acids and glycerol into the blood so that other tissues can use fatty acids as fuel. The reasoning runs from the hormonal state (low insulin = fasting) to the metabolic direction (mobilization, not storage).

(Choice B) is correct: low insulin activates HSL, which hydrolyzes stored TAG and releases fatty acids plus glycerol into circulation. That is the defining event of fasting-state fat mobilization.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the regulation. High insulin (the fed state) suppresses HSL and favors fat storage; when insulin has fallen, HSL is active and fat is mobilized, not retained.
(Choice C) is incorrect: lipoprotein lipase loading fatty acids for storage is favored by insulin in the fed state. In fasting with low insulin, the adipocyte is releasing fat, not taking it up for storage.
(Choice D) is incorrect: chylomicron assembly is a feature of dietary fat absorption in enterocytes after a meal, not of adipocyte behavior during fasting; it does not describe the fasting hormonal response.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, it requires the student to connect the fed/fasting hormonal signal to the direction of adipose metabolism rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '62d14d91-b276-4753-a3e4-2a6fc1af478a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is A because free energy is not transmitted between two reactions just because they share a beaker. For the \(-30.5\) kJ/mol of ATP hydrolysis to actually drive the \(+15\) kJ/mol conversion, the reactions must be mechanistically connected through a SHARED INTERMEDIATE (or a common enzyme/active site). Typically ATP transfers its phosphate onto one of the reactants, creating a phosphorylated intermediate that carries the energy forward; the connected sequence then has a single summed \(\Delta G\) (about \(-15.5\) kJ/mol here), which is negative and therefore spontaneous.

(Choice A) is correct: it identifies the shared/phosphorylated intermediate as the physical linkage that lets one reaction's favorability be transmitted to the other, yielding a net-negative pathway.

(Choice B) is incorrect: two thermodynamically favorable-on-paper numbers do not add unless the reactions are actually coupled. Without a shared intermediate, ATP hydrolysis simply proceeds on its own and X → Y stays unfavorable. The \(\Delta G\) values do not spontaneously combine.

(Choice C) is incorrect: shared temperature and pressure do not couple reactions and do not equalize free-energy changes; each reaction retains its own \(\Delta G\). Constant T and P are just the conditions under which \(\Delta G\) predicts spontaneity, not a coupling mechanism.

(Choice D) is incorrect: coupling is not a thermal effect. The heat from ATP hydrolysis is not what drives the endergonic reaction; energy is transmitted chemically through the shared intermediate, not by warming the solvent.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: applying the additivity of \(\Delta G\) along a linked reaction pathway to recognize that thermodynamic coupling requires a shared chemical intermediate, not mere spatial proximity.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"The two reactions must share a common chemical intermediate (for instance, ATP transfers its phosphate to X, forming a phosphorylated species that then reacts onward), so the linked pathway carries a single, net-negative \\(\\Delta G\\).","label":"A"},{"text":"As long as ATP hydrolysis releases more free energy than X → Y requires, the two reactions in the same solution will automatically sum to a negative \\(\\Delta G\\) without any physical link between them.","label":"B"},{"text":"Coupling requires only that both reactions occur at the same temperature and pressure, which equalizes their free-energy changes and forces X → Y forward.","label":"C"},{"text":"The heat released by exergonic ATP hydrolysis warms the surrounding solution, and that added thermal energy is what pushes the endergonic reaction over its barrier.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '635a9137-3789-4913-9429-eb617d9ea3d0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This Biosignaling item requires predicting the output of a G protein-coupled receptor cascade when the intrinsic timing switch of the \(\alpha\) subunit is broken. In the normal cycle, an agonist-bound receptor catalyzes GDP-for-GTP exchange on \(G\alpha\); the GTP-bound \(\alpha\) subunit dissociates from \(\beta\gamma\) and activates its effector, and signaling is terminated only when the subunit's own intrinsic GTPase hydrolyzes GTP back to GDP, restoring the inactive \(\alpha\)-GDP state that reassociates with \(\beta\gamma\). Because hydrolysis is the sole reset step, it (not the presence of hormone) sets how long the effector stays on. (Choice A) is correct: with hydrolysis abolished but GTP binding and effector activation intact, the pulse loads the mutant \(\alpha\) subunit with GTP, and it cannot return to the off state; it therefore keeps stimulating adenylyl cyclase long after the hormone is washed away and the receptor has reset (the same logic underlies cholera toxin's locking of \(G_s\alpha\) in its active form). (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the mechanism. The lesion blocks GTP hydrolysis, not GTP binding, and it is the GTP-loaded (not GDP-loaded) state that dissociates from \(\beta\gamma\), so the mutant signals longer, not shorter. (Choice C) is incorrect: the stem specifies that GTP binding and effector activation are unaffected, so the subunit still dissociates and activates the cyclase during the pulse; the defect lies only in turning the signal off. (Choice D) is incorrect: normal return to baseline depends on GTP hydrolysis to end the signal, which is exactly the step this mutant lacks, so the effector cannot shut off on the usual timescale. Skill 2: the student must apply the GTP/GDP switch mechanism to a novel mutant scenario and predict the downstream consequence rather than recall a stated fact.$px$ WHERE id = '640b3745-7cbd-4d88-88a6-6026336dfb6a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' The extracellular matrix does more than hold cells together into tissues; it also lets cells sense their surroundings and change their behavior in response. When a molecule in the matrix binds a receptor on the plasma membrane's extracellular surface, it alters the receptor's molecular structure. The receptor in turn changes the conformation of the microfilaments positioned just inside the plasma membrane, and these conformational changes trigger chemical signals within the cell that reach the nucleus and switch transcription of specific DNA sections on or off, thereby affecting production of the associated proteins. The key point is that the message is relayed inward by conformational change and intracellular signaling, not by the matrix protein physically entering the cell. (Choice A) is correct: the ligand-bound receptor changes shape, this remodels the cytoskeletal microfilaments just inside the membrane, and the resulting intracellular signals reach the nucleus to turn transcription on or off. That exactly matches the described outcome in which the matrix protein never enters the cell. (Choice B) is incorrect: the stem specifies that the matrix protein never enters the cell, so it cannot be carried in to bind DNA directly; the receptor transmits the signal without importing the ligand. (Choice C) is incorrect: gap junctions pass ions and small molecules between adjacent cells, but the described response occurs within the cell that binds the matrix protein and involves the nucleus of that same cell, not transfer through a junction to a neighbor. (Choice D) is incorrect: the matrix protein is a large polar molecule that cannot slip through the hydrophobic lipid bilayer on its own, which is precisely why a surface receptor is needed to relay its signal inward. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of extracellular-matrix signaling (surface binding, receptor conformational change, and intracellular relay to the nucleus) to a novel experiment rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '64b14ea5-8a68-4f04-9967-0734f47a3250';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because the tyrosine side chain is a phenol (a hydroxyl group bonded directly to an aromatic ring), and tyrosine is grouped with phenylalanine and tryptophan as one of the three aromatic amino acids. The three aromatic amino acids (phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) all contain an aromatic functional group, and the hydroxyl group on tyrosine increases its reactivity and solubility relative to phenylalanine, confirming that the phenol sits on a ring system. Reading each row's listed functional group and assigning the class shows that only the tyrosine row matches the standard scheme. (Choice A) Serine carries a hydroxyl side chain and belongs with the hydroxyl-containing amino acids serine and threonine (along with cysteine) in the polar uncharged class. It is polar uncharged, not nonpolar, so the serine row is mislabeled. (Choice B) Glutamate's carboxylate side chain makes it acidic; glutamate (glutamic acid) and aspartate (aspartic acid) are the two acidic amino acids, so labeling it basic reverses its actual class. (Choice C) is correct: the phenol-bearing tyrosine is correctly placed in the aromatic class. (Choice D) Histidine is one of the basic amino acids, together with lysine and arginine, so its imidazole side chain places it in the basic class, not polar uncharged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the classification rules to the structural feature listed in each table row to determine which amino acid is paired with the correct polarity class.$px$ WHERE id = '651a673b-a143-4994-abfc-f3fbd6f5075b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is D because the two halves of a triacylglycerol have different metabolic fates. The glycerol backbone travels to the liver, is phosphorylated to glycerol-3-phosphate, and enters gluconeogenesis. Glycerol is glucogenic. The fatty acids are broken down to acetyl-CoA, and acetyl-CoA cannot be converted to a net gain of glucose in humans. So only the glycerol portion can raise blood glucose.

(Choice D) is correct: glycerol enters gluconeogenesis and yields net glucose, while the fatty-acyl portion is converted to acetyl-CoA, which provides no net glucose.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it inverts the two fates. Fatty acids become acetyl-CoA, which is not a net glucose source; it is the glycerol, not the fatty acids, that is glucogenic.
(Choice B) is incorrect: fatty-acid-derived acetyl-CoA does not produce net glucose, so not every carbon of the triacylglycerol can be rebuilt into glucose.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it ignores glycerol's glucogenic role. Glycerol genuinely can be converted to glucose, so the products are not limited to ATP production alone.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, it requires tracing each fragment of the molecule to its distinct metabolic endpoint rather than recalling a single fact about fat.$px$ WHERE id = '654c9081-a869-4aa3-8faa-723c83edc26a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is D because whole-body fuel metabolism is set by the insulin-to-glucagon ratio, \( \text{insulin}{:}\text{glucagon} \). A high ratio (the fed sample) is an anabolic signal: liver and muscle take up glucose to build glycogen and fat and to synthesize protein. A low ratio means glucagon (with epinephrine) dominates relative to insulin. That is an unambiguous catabolic signal. The liver responds by mobilizing fuel: glycogenolysis first, then gluconeogenesis from glycerol, lactate, and glucogenic amino acids, while adipose lipolysis releases fatty acids for oxidation. So the sharp fall in the ratio points whole-body metabolism toward mobilization and glucose export, exactly as option A describes.

(Choice D) is correct: a low \( \text{insulin}{:}\text{glucagon} \) ratio is a catabolic signal, so the liver runs glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis while adipose tissue undergoes lipolysis. The net effect is fuel mobilization.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reads the low ratio as a signal of abundance and predicts anabolic storage (glycogen and fat synthesis). That is the response to a HIGH ratio in the fed state; a low ratio drives the opposite direction.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it has the liver building glycogen and running gluconeogenesis at the same time. These opposing programs are reciprocally controlled by the ratio. At a low ratio glycogen synthesis is switched off while glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis are switched on.

(Choice C) is incorrect: a low ratio does not drive net protein synthesis or fuel storage. Fasting is catabolic. Amino acids are released and used as gluconeogenic substrate, not consumed to store surplus fuel.

This is a Skill 2 (Reasoning About Scientific Principles) item because it asks you to reason from a described hormonal ratio to the dominant direction of whole-body metabolism.$px$ WHERE id = '65c104bd-4adb-40ae-941e-c75ff8587a4d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because two stereoisomers that differ at some (but not all) of their chiral centers are non-superimposable and are not mirror images of one another; by definition, stereoisomers that are not mirror images are diastereomers. Here Sugar P and Sugar Q share four chiral centers, differ at two of them (C3 and C5), and match at the other two (C2 and C4). Because only some centers are inverted, the molecules cannot be mirror images, yet they are distinct stereoisomers. That is precisely the definition of diastereomers.

(Choice D) is correct: Diastereomers are stereoisomers that are not mirror images of each other. Differing at exactly two of four centers guarantees the sugars are non-superimposable but not mirror images, so "diastereomers" is the most precise classification.

(Choice B) is incorrect: Enantiomers are stereoisomers that ARE non-superimposable mirror images. A mirror-image relationship requires the configuration at EVERY chiral center to be inverted, which here would mean all four. Because C2 and C4 are unchanged, the two sugars cannot be mirror images and therefore cannot be enantiomers.

(Choice C) is incorrect: Epimers are the special subset of diastereomers that differ at exactly ONE chiral center (e.g., glucose and galactose at C4). Sugar P and Sugar Q differ at two centers, so the more restrictive term "epimers" does not apply even though they are diastereomers.

(Choice A) is incorrect: Anomers are diastereomers of CYCLIC sugars that differ only at the new hemiacetal/acetal (anomeric) carbon generated upon ring closure. These sugars are described in the open-chain form and differ at C3 and C5, not at an anomeric carbon, so "anomers" is inapplicable.

Skill 2: Rather than recalling a definition, you had to translate the described pattern (differs at two of four centers, matches at two) into structural consequences (non-superimposable but not a mirror image) and then select the classification whose defining criterion that pattern uniquely satisfies while ruling out the more restrictive terms (enantiomer, epimer, anomer).$px$ WHERE id = '662825fd-5b65-4153-8acf-f820572babe8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the equivalence point of a strong acid-strong base titration is exactly neutral at 25 °C. At the equivalence point the moles of \(\text{NaOH}\) added equal the moles of \(\text{HCl}\) present: \((0.100\ \text{M})(0.0250\ \text{L}) = 2.50 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\) of each, so all \(\text{HCl}\) is consumed and neither acid nor base is in excess. The only species remaining besides water is the salt \(\text{NaCl}\). Because \(\text{Na}^+\) is the cation of a strong base and \(\text{Cl}^-\) is the anion of a strong acid, neither ion hydrolyzes, so the salt is pH-neutral and the pH is fixed solely by water autoionization: \([\text{H}^+] = [\text{OH}^-] = 1.0 \times 10^{-7}\ \text{M}\), giving \(\text{pH} = -\log(1.0 \times 10^{-7}) = 7.00\). (Choice A) \(\text{pH} = 1.00\) is \(-\log(0.100)\), the pH of the original \(\text{HCl}\) before any base is added; it ignores that the titration has reached the equivalence point and the acid has been fully neutralized. (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 4.00\) assumes the salt hydrolyzes to give an acidic solution, but \(\text{Cl}^-\) is the conjugate base of a strong acid and is far too weak to hydrolyze, so no acidity is generated. (Choice C) \(\text{pH} = 13.00\) treats the titrant base as if it were in excess, computing \(\text{pOH} = -\log(0.100) = 1.00\) and \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - 1.00 = 13.00\); at the exact equivalence point, however, the base is not in excess. Stoichiometric amounts have reacted. Skill 1$px$ WHERE id = '66fef40a-f13c-4ed2-94f2-3f74af455eb9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Skeletal muscle tension is graded partly by the frequency of motor-neuron firing: if a second stimulus arrives while the fiber is still contracting from the first, the two responses add together in wave summation, releasing more \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and activating additional sarcomeres so the recorded tension exceeds a single twitch. The critical comparison is between the inter-stimulus interval and the full duration of one twitch. A twitch here lasts 90 ms from stimulus to complete return to baseline, so any stimulus arriving less than 90 ms after the previous one lands before relaxation is finished and will summate; any interval of 90 ms or longer lets the fiber relax fully first, producing separate, non-summing twitches of unchanged amplitude. (Choice C) is correct: 70 ms is the longest listed interval that is still shorter than the 90 ms twitch, so it is the first protocol (as the interval shortens from W to Z) in which the second stimulus arrives during the ongoing twitch and tension summates above a single twitch. (Choice A) is incorrect: at 120 ms the fiber has fully relaxed (well past 90 ms) before the next stimulus, so each twitch is discrete and peak tension equals a single twitch. (Choice B) is incorrect: 100 ms still exceeds the 90 ms twitch duration, so relaxation completes and no summation occurs; a student who assumes any rapid repetition summates may pick the first interval under a nice round threshold rather than comparing to the actual twitch duration. (Choice D) is incorrect: 30 ms does produce strong summation and even approaches tetanus, but the question asks for the first protocol that exceeds a single twitch as the interval shortens, and summation already begins at the longer 70 ms interval, so Z is not the first. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of wave summation (comparing the inter-stimulus interval to the full twitch duration) to determine which stimulation pattern first raises tension above a single twitch.$px$ WHERE id = '67418d80-e84c-4a6c-a669-601d3c0cb7ff';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Ions in Solutions (GC).' The conductivity of an aqueous solution rises with the concentration of freely mobile ions it contains: to conduct current, a solution must hold dissolved, charged species, and more charge carriers per liter means greater conductivity. Because all four solutes are at the same \(0.10\ \text{M}\) formula-unit concentration, the deciding factor is how many mobile ions each formula unit releases upon complete dissociation. (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{FeCl}_3\) dissociates into \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) plus \(3\,\text{Cl}^{-}\), yielding 4 ions per formula unit, so \(0.10\ \text{M}\ \text{FeCl}_3\) produces \(0.40\ \text{M}\) total ions. That is the largest concentration of charge carriers and therefore the highest conductivity. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{MgCl}_2\) gives \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) plus \(2\,\text{Cl}^{-}\), only 3 ions per formula unit (\(0.30\ \text{M}\) ions); the higher \(2+\) charge on \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) does not compensate, because conductivity here tracks the number of mobile ions produced, and \(\text{MgCl}_2\) produces fewer than \(\text{FeCl}_3\). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{NaCl}\) is the most familiar strong electrolyte, but it releases only \(\text{Na}^{+}\) and \(\text{Cl}^{-}\), 2 ions per formula unit (\(0.20\ \text{M}\) ions), so at equal molarity it conducts less than the salts that liberate more ions. (Choice D) is incorrect: glucose is a nonelectrolyte that dissolves as intact neutral molecules and releases no ions at all, so its solution has essentially no charge carriers and the lowest conductivity, not the highest. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between conductivity and mobile-ion concentration to compare solutes that differ in how many ions each formula unit releases.$px$ WHERE id = '6766e542-e22a-46e3-aa30-055457a88811';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because lysosomal hydrolases are only active at the low pH the proton pump maintains, so neutralizing the lumen leaves them inactive and substrate undigested. MAIN REASONING: The enzymes within lysosomes break down proteins, polysaccharides, lipids, nucleic acids, and worn-out organelles, and these enzymes are active at a much lower pH than the cytoplasm, so the lysosomal lumen is more acidic than the surrounding cytoplasm. The acidic lumen is generated by an active proton pump; if that pump is blocked, the lumen drifts toward the cytoplasm's near-neutral pH \((\sim 7)\), which is outside the operating range of the acid hydrolases. Many reactions that occur in the cytoplasm could not proceed at a low pH, which underscores that these enzymes are pH-tuned to their compartment, so removing the acidity shuts down digestion and engulfed substrate piles up rather than being broken down. (Choice A) is correct: the loss of digestion follows directly from inactivation of pH-dependent hydrolases, not from any change to the enzymes' structure; raising the pump back would restore activity, which is the signature of reversible loss of activity rather than destruction. (Choice B) reverses the relationship. The hydrolases require the low pH, so a higher pH suppresses rather than enhances their activity; the claim that acidity "inhibits" them inverts the actual pH optimum. (Choice C) is a partial truth taken too far: digestion does halt, but the enzymes are inactivated by being outside their pH optimum, not permanently denatured and destroyed; lysosomal function depends on a low-pH requirement, not on irreversible destruction of the proteins. (Choice D) imports the wrong organelle: peroxisomes carry out oxidation of fatty acids and amino acids and detoxify \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\), and they do not substitute for lysosomal hydrolytic digestion of engulfed macromolecules, so no peroxisome-driven rescue or export occurs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the proton pump's role in acidifying the lumen with the hydrolases' pH dependence to predict the functional consequence of the inhibitor.$px$ WHERE id = '676f2536-7607-44ba-8ad3-31c285f61729';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. The carbonic acid-bicarbonate buffer couples blood pH to ventilation through \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\). The answer to this question is C because exhaling \(\text{CO}_2\) lowers \(\text{PCO}_2\), and by Le Chatelier's principle the system replaces the lost \(\text{CO}_2\) by shifting toward the left, a direction that consumes \(\text{H}^+\); a falling \([\text{H}^+]\) is by definition a rising pH, moving the acidemic blood back toward 7.4. Tracing the reaction: removing the rightmost-feeding species \(\text{CO}_2\) depletes \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), which is regenerated from \(\text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\) recombining; each recombination removes one free \(\text{H}^+\), so \([\text{H}^+]\) drops and pH \(= -\log[\text{H}^+]\) climbs. This is the appropriate respiratory compensation a healthy ventilatory drive mounts against a metabolic (non-respiratory) acid load. (Choice A) This reverses the direction of the shift: lowering a reactant-side species pulls the equilibrium toward that side (left), not toward the products, so \(\text{H}^+\) is consumed rather than generated and the pH rises rather than falls. (Choice B) Hyperventilation removes \(\text{CO}_2\) from the blood rather than adding it; while increased ventilation does raise \(\text{O}_2\) uptake, \(\text{O}_2\) is not part of this buffer equilibrium, and the net effect of blowing off \(\text{CO}_2\) is a fall, not a rise, in \([\text{H}^+]\). (Choice D) This correctly identifies a leftward shift but draws the wrong conclusion about pH: a leftward shift consumes \(\text{H}^+\), and a lower \([\text{H}^+]\) raises pH; the transient decrease in \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\) does not acidify the blood because it is accompanied by net removal of free protons. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = '67b733fe-756d-49de-a389-943391f1cdf8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is A because a glucose polymer has exactly one reducing end (the single anchored terminus) but one non-reducing end for every chain tip, so each additional branch point creates one additional non-reducing end. The exo-acting enzyme described removes residues one at a time only from these non-reducing termini, and because several copies act on one molecule simultaneously, the rate of glucose release scales with the number of non-reducing ends. In the schematic, structure B carries more branch points than structure A, so B presents more non-reducing ends for simultaneous attack and therefore releases glucose faster. This is why a densely branched storage polymer can be cleaved one residue at a time from all of its branches at once (at the many non-reducing ends), liberating a large amount of free glucose quickly.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the topology read from the figure. Structure A is the less-branched of the two, so it has fewer chain termini, not more; the functional claim is right but it is attached to the wrong structure.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it identifies structure B correctly but mislabels the extra termini as reducing ends. Branching multiplies non-reducing ends only; any glucose polymer, no matter how branched, still has just one reducing end (the anchored end), so extra branches cannot add reducing ends. The stem also specifies that the enzyme acts away from the anchored end, so "anchored reducing ends" is the wrong site entirely.

(Choice A) is correct: structure B is the more densely branched polymer in the schematic, so it presents the greater number of non-reducing chain termini, and because copies of the exo-acting enzyme work on those termini at the same time, glucose is liberated more rapidly.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly picks structure B and correctly notes more termini, but it inverts the functional consequence. More simultaneous attack sites speed release rather than slow it; this is precisely why animals store glucose as a highly branched polymer for rapid mobilization.

At the Skill 2 level, the task is not to recall a definition but to read branching density directly from the figure, translate branch points into a count of non-reducing termini, and connect that count to the rate of an exo-acting degradative enzyme, applying the structure-function relationship rather than restating it.$px$ WHERE id = '67b8cd23-5479-48ec-a4b8-bf4b8a19ac88';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because in maltose the α-1,4 bond ties up the anomeric carbon (C1) of only the first glucose; the second glucose keeps its C1 as a free hemiacetal. A hemiacetal reversibly opens to the open-chain aldehyde, and that single equilibrium accounts for both observations at once. The transient open-chain form lets the ring re-close as either the α- or β-anomer, so a freshly dissolved single anomer relaxes toward the equilibrium anomer mixture and the optical rotation drifts to a stable value (mutarotation). The same aldehyde is oxidized by Cu²⁺, reducing it to brick-red Cu₂O, which is the positive Benedict's result. Sucrose lacks any free anomeric carbon (its Glc(α1,2β)Fru bond joins both anomeric carbons), so it cannot open, cannot mutarotate, and is non-reducing.

(Choice B) is correct: a single free hemiacetal (one free anomeric carbon) is exactly what is needed to open to an aldehyde, and that one feature explains both mutarotation and the positive Benedict's test.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it wrongly claims both anomeric carbons are used by the α-1,4 bond. A 1,4 linkage only consumes one anomeric carbon (C1 of the first residue) plus the C4-OH of the second; the second residue's anomeric carbon stays free, so no special basic conditions are required to expose the aldehyde. This choice essentially describes sucrose, not maltose.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the α versus β designation refers to which face the glycosidic oxygen points on the first residue and does not "lock" the second residue's ring. The shifting rotation comes from ring opening and re-closing at the free anomeric carbon (mutarotation), not from hydrolysis of the disaccharide; hydrolysis is far slower and is not what produces mutarotation.

(Choice D) is incorrect: only the residue whose anomeric carbon is not engaged in the glycosidic bond can open to an aldehyde. The first glucose's C1 is locked as a full acetal by the α-1,4 bond, so it cannot open; maltose therefore has exactly one reducing end, not two.

Skill 2 is exercised here by requiring you to reason from a described linkage (α-1,4, one anomeric carbon free) to the predicted chemical behavior (mutarotation and reducing activity) and to reject the parallel but incorrect claims that either both or neither anomeric carbon is available.$px$ WHERE id = '67c2217e-ad85-4cad-aed9-0e0b0066ddb3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because under normal-to-moderate conditions, arterial CO2 is the primary drive for breathing: rising PCO2 diffuses across the blood-brain barrier into the cerebrospinal fluid, where it forms carbonic acid and lowers CSF pH; central chemoreceptors in the medulla detect this drop in pH and increase the output of the medullary rhythm generator. This automatic drive eventually overrides the voluntary (cortical) suppression of breathing, forcing an involuntary inspiration even while PO2 remains well above the ~60 mmHg threshold at which hypoxic drive becomes significant. The hyperventilation beforehand blew off CO2, delaying (but not preventing) the CO2-driven breakpoint.

(Choice C) is correct: it correctly identifies rising CO2 leading to lower CSF pH, central chemoreceptor stimulation, and increased medullary rhythm generator output overriding cortical inhibition, which is the dominant and correct mechanism at these blood-gas values.

(Choice A) is incorrect: central chemoreceptors respond to CO2/H+ in the CSF, not to O2; they are not oxygen sensors, and they do not 'directly inhibit the cortex.' The medullary generator overrides voluntary suppression by driving inspiration, not by inhibiting the cortex.

(Choice B) is incorrect: at a PO2 of 62 mmHg the hypoxic (peripheral) drive is still minor; peripheral chemoreceptors respond strongly to hypoxia only below roughly 60 mmHg. CO2 remains the primary breakpoint stimulus here, so hypoxia is not yet dominant.

(Choice D) is incorrect: rising CO2 lowers (acidifies) CSF pH rather than raising it, and it is the CO2-driven acidosis sensed centrally, not an aortic-arch alkalosis signal, that triggers the breath; the direction of the pH change is reversed.

This item tests the skill of integrating chemoreceptor feedback with neural rhythm generation to reason from blood-gas data to the mechanism that overrides voluntary breath-holding.$px$ WHERE id = '6841b8a5-fc78-4f76-b1d1-2ba035814501';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher analyzes a purified protein by SDS-PAGE. The intact assembly has an apparent mass of 65 kDa and is built from one copy each of two different polypeptides, chain A (40 kDa) and chain B (25 kDa), which are held together by an inter-chain S-S linkage between a cysteine on A and a cysteine on B. The researcher prepares two identical samples: sample 1 is heated in SDS loading buffer alone, and sample 2 is heated in SDS loading buffer to which dithiothreitol (DTT) has been added. Both samples are then loaded onto the same gel and run against molecular-weight standards. Which band pattern is observed for sample 1 (SDS only) versus sample 2 (SDS + DTT)?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.' The answer is A because SDS is a detergent that denatures the protein and coats it in negative charge by disrupting noncovalent interactions, but it does not break covalent bonds. The inter-chain disulfide is a covalent S-S bond, so in sample 1 (SDS only) chains A and B stay covalently tethered and migrate together as one 65 kDa species. Only in sample 2, where the reducing agent DTT is present, is the disulfide cleaved, releasing chain A and chain B to migrate independently at 40 kDa and 25 kDa. Because SDS makes mobility a function of mass alone (\(\ln M_r\) is linear in \(R_f\)), each freed chain resolves at its own molecular weight.

(Choice A) is correct: without a reducing agent the covalent disulfide survives, giving one 65 kDa band; adding DTT cleaves it, splitting the assembly into 40 kDa and 25 kDa bands.

(Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the roles of the two lanes. It assumes SDS alone separates the chains and that adding a reducing agent somehow re-links them, but reducing agents cleave disulfides rather than forming them, so the SDS-only lane cannot be the one that shows separated subunits.

(Choice C) is incorrect: this treats SDS itself as capable of breaking the disulfide, so both lanes would show separated subunits. SDS disrupts only noncovalent interactions; the covalent S-S bond persists unless a reducing agent is added, so the SDS-only lane must show a single 65 kDa band.

(Choice D) is incorrect: this assumes the disulfide is never broken under either condition. DTT is a reducing agent added specifically in sample 2, and it does cleave the inter-chain disulfide, so sample 2 must resolve into two subunit bands rather than remaining one 65 kDa band.

By predicting the outcome of a described procedure from the chemical nature of the bonds involved, this question requires reasoning about the design and execution of a research protocol (Skill 2) rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '68fff828-61c5-44ca-8b90-1f023d004a80';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' Prokaryotes reproduce asexually by binary fission: the single circular chromosome is replicated and the enlarged cell is pinched inward at its equator so that the two resulting cells, which are clones, separate. This process does not provide any opportunity for genetic recombination or genetic diversity. Because every division yields clones, a colony grown from one founder is genetically uniform, and any new trait such as ampicillin resistance in a few rare cells must originate outside the division process itself, either from a spontaneous mutation or from DNA acquired by transformation, transduction, or conjugation. (Choice A) is correct: it correctly frames the division as a clonal, variation-free copying event and correctly attributes the rare resistant survivors to a separate source (mutation or acquired DNA), matching both observations. (Choice B) is incorrect: prokaryotes do not divide by mitosis, and there is no crossing over between homologous chromosomes because a bacterium carries a single chromosome, not homologous pairs, so this cannot generate the survivors. (Choice C) is incorrect: fission produces genetically identical daughters, not routinely differing ones; a parent bacterium does not carry "two chromosome copies" to assort in the way described, and treating the resistant cells as ordinary variants contradicts the fact that they are rare within a uniform colony. (Choice D) is incorrect: conjugation transfers DNA between cells through a pilus but it is horizontal gene transfer, not the division that builds the colony; if a resistance plasmid had been handed to every daughter, the whole colony would be resistant rather than only a rare few. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that binary fission is clonal (producing no genetic variation) to conclude that the colony's uniformity comes from the division while the rare resistant cells must come from an independent source such as mutation or acquired DNA.$px$ WHERE id = '690f625a-2025-4f33-aa5f-cb6dd3290bbc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because central chemoreceptors respond to the H+ (pH) of the CSF, which is set by CSF PCO2 and CSF bicarbonate. Acutely, hypoxia-driven hyperventilation blows off CO2, lowering CSF PCO2 and making the CSF alkaline; this rise in CSF pH suppresses the central receptors and blunts drive. Over days, the choroid plexus actively transports bicarbonate out of the CSF, lowering CSF [HCO3-] so that CSF pH returns toward normal despite the persistently low PCO2. With the alkaline brake removed, the central chemoreceptors resume firing and ventilation climbs. This is the basis of ventilatory acclimatization. (Choice D) is correct: lowering CSF bicarbonate offsets the low CSF PCO2, normalizing CSF pH and restoring central chemoreceptor drive. (Choice A) is incorrect: raising bicarbonate would make the CSF more alkaline, which suppresses rather than stimulates the central chemoreceptors, and these receptors are stimulated by acidity (H+), not alkalinity. (Choice B) is incorrect: at altitude ventilation stays elevated, so CSF PCO2 remains below its sea-level value; it does not rise back above baseline, and re-acidification comes from lowered bicarbonate, not from rising CO2. (Choice C) is incorrect: central chemoreceptors do not sense oxygen; the peripheral chemoreceptors handle the hypoxic drive, and CSF PO2 changes do not directly activate the central receptors. This item tests the skill of reasoning about how a regulatory setpoint is reset through a slow compensatory adjustment (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving).$px$ WHERE id = '69451890-667d-4cda-89ef-50f17390a8e6';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher purifies a recombinant esterase on an affinity column whose beads carry a covalently attached small-molecule inhibitor that occupies the enzyme's active site. After the sample is loaded and contaminants are washed away, the bound esterase is split across four identical columns, and a different elution buffer is applied to each. The researcher measures the amount of protein recovered from each column and the specific activity (units of esterase activity per milligram) of that recovered protein:

Column 1 (buffer at pH 2.5): 95% of protein recovered; specific activity 4 U/mg
Column 2 (buffer containing 6 M guanidinium chloride): 97% of protein recovered; specific activity 2 U/mg
Column 3 (buffer with a high concentration of the same free inhibitor added): 88% of protein recovered; specific activity 210 U/mg
Column 4 (buffer with elevated NaCl at 1 M): 15% of protein recovered; specific activity 205 U/mg

The researcher needs the largest possible quantity of catalytically active esterase. Which elution condition best meets this goal, and why?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.'

The answer is A because the goal is the largest quantity of *active* enzyme, which is the product of how much protein comes off the column and how much of that protein is still catalytically competent (its specific activity). The bead-attached ligand is an active-site inhibitor, so the enzyme is held by the same specific interaction that a free inhibitor molecule would make. Flooding the column with excess free inhibitor sets up a competition: free ligand in solution mass-competes the enzyme off the immobilized ligand, releasing it under mild, non-denaturing conditions. Column 3 recovers 88% of the protein at 210 U/mg, by far the highest activity per milligram, so the total active yield (recovery × specific activity) is the greatest of the four options. The competitive strategy displaces the target while leaving its native fold, and therefore its catalytic site, intact.

(Choice A) is correct: competitive elution with excess free ligand releases the enzyme by out-competing the immobilized ligand for the active site, a gentle process that leaves the protein folded and active (210 U/mg), and the 88% recovery keeps the total active yield high.

(Choice B) is incorrect: pH 2.5 does strip nearly all the protein off (95%), but the specific activity collapses to 4 U/mg. Extreme low pH disrupts the folded structure of most enzymes, so although the mass recovery is high, almost none of the recovered protein is catalytically active. High protein yield is not the same as high active yield.

(Choice C) is incorrect: 6 M guanidinium chloride is a chaotropic denaturant. It efficiently removes protein (97%) precisely because it unfolds the enzyme and thereby destroys the binding interaction, but the same unfolding destroys the active site (2 U/mg). Maximizing protein recovered while sacrificing structure defeats the stated goal of recovering active enzyme.

(Choice D) is incorrect: raising ionic strength preserves activity (205 U/mg) but is the wrong lever for an affinity interaction based on a specific active-site fit rather than on ionic attraction. That is why only 15% of the protein is recovered. Most of it stays stuck to the column. High salt is the elution mechanism for ion-exchange (charge-based) resins, not for a ligand that binds a defined structural pocket, so the total active yield is low.

To recover the most active protein, you must reason across both columns of data at once, combining recovery with per-milligram activity to judge total active yield, rather than optimizing either number alone.$px$ WHERE id = '6948da4a-d374-4775-8839-276784f031ba';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A patient's blood is drawn 90 minutes after a large mixed meal, and insulin is high. Considering insulin's coordinated actions on lipid metabolism (its effects on lipoprotein lipase, adipose lipolysis, hepatic and adipose lipogenesis, and ketone-body formation), which set of changes best describes the direction of lipid handling in this fed, high-insulin state?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is A because a high insulin level signals the fed state, and insulin directs lipids toward storage on every front. It raises lipoprotein lipase activity so that triacylglycerols in circulating VLDL and chylomicrons are hydrolyzed and their fatty acids taken up; it promotes triacylglycerol synthesis (lipogenesis) in adipose tissue and liver; it suppresses adipose lipolysis so stored fat is not released; and it lowers hepatic ketone-body formation. Together these move lipid into storage.

(Choice A) is correct: increased lipoprotein lipase, promoted lipogenesis, suppressed lipolysis, and reduced ketogenesis are the coordinated fed-state actions, and their net direction is fat storage.

(Choice B) is incorrect: suppressed lipoprotein lipase, accelerated lipolysis, and increased ketogenesis are the low-insulin, high-glucagon fasting pattern of fat mobilization, the opposite of what a high-insulin state produces.

(Choice C) is incorrect: insulin increases, not inhibits, lipoprotein lipase; leaving VLDL and chylomicron triacylglycerols uncleared would contradict insulin's role in delivering those fatty acids to tissues for storage.

(Choice D) is incorrect: insulin lowers ketone-body formation. It would be self-contradictory for the liver to store fat while simultaneously oxidizing it to ketones; ketogenesis rises in the fasting state, not the fed state.

This question rewards Skill 2 (reasoning): from a described high-insulin state you had to deduce the coordinated direction of lipid handling across several tissues, not restate a definition.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"Lipoprotein lipase activity is increased (clearing triacylglycerols from circulating VLDL and chylomicrons), triacylglycerol synthesis (lipogenesis) is promoted in adipose and liver, adipose lipolysis is suppressed, and hepatic ketone-body formation is lowered; the net direction is fat storage.","label":"A"},{"text":"Lipoprotein lipase is suppressed, adipose lipolysis is accelerated to release free fatty acids, and the liver ramps up ketone-body production; the net direction is fat mobilization to supply fuel.","label":"B"},{"text":"Adipose lipolysis is suppressed and lipogenesis is promoted, but lipoprotein lipase is simultaneously inhibited so that circulating VLDL and chylomicron triacylglycerols are left in the blood rather than cleared.","label":"C"},{"text":"Lipoprotein lipase is increased and lipogenesis promoted, but hepatic ketone-body formation also rises because the newly stored fat is immediately oxidized in the liver to ketones.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '6951106d-bb42-45fb-8bf9-2d05ad627ad5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is C because an oncogene is precisely a gene that, once altered, leads to an increase in the rate of cell-cycle progression, which matches the scenario of an altered protein pushing the cell through checkpoints faster and driving uncontrolled division. Genes coding for positive cell-cycle regulators are called proto-oncogenes, and when mutated they become oncogenes, genes that drive a cell toward becoming cancerous. An oncogene is any gene that, when altered, increases the rate of cell-cycle progression. Because the gene described started as a positive regulator and its mutation accelerates the cycle, its mutated form is by definition an oncogene rather than its un-mutated precursor or a negative regulator. (Choice A) is incorrect because a tumor suppressor gene codes for negative regulators that prevent the cell from undergoing uncontrolled division; it is the opposite gene class, and cancer arises from its loss, not its gain of activity. (Choice B) is incorrect because the proto-oncogene is the normal, un-mutated precursor that is harmless until altered, whereas the scenario describes the gene after it has already been mutated. (Choice C) is correct because the mutated positive regulator that speeds cell-cycle progression is exactly what defines an oncogene. (Choice D) is incorrect because a mutated negative regulator unable to signal DNA-repair enzymes describes a damaged tumor suppressor such as p53, not an oncogene. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling and applying the definition of an oncogene as the mutated form of a proto-oncogene that increases cell-cycle progression.$px$ WHERE id = '697cc046-427a-4880-a893-2435f49c7e0e';

COMMIT;
